WEBVTT


00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:10.844
- There was nothing
extraordinary about the fact


00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:13.013
That you lose the people
that you love,


00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:16.049
'Cause it's gonna happen
to all of us.


00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:19.953
It's just that it happened
in this targeted community


00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:22.989
Of people
who were disenfranchised


00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:26.026
And separated
from their families,


00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:29.496
And a whole group
of other people stepped up


00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:32.966
And became their family.


00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:37.737
- We are not
some network of people


00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:39.039
Who just like to have sex.


00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:42.075
We are not some
ephemeral subculture


00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:44.244
That comes
and dissolves and goes.


00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.846
This is a community
that was tested


00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:49.883
In a way almost no community
on earth is ever tested,


00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:51.618
And succeeded
in what it was trying to do,


00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.220
Which is save as many lives
of people as it could,


00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.522
Stop civil rights attacks,


00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.257
And then
try to use that example


00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:59.426
To transform the world.


00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:01.594
If you're ever facing
a natural disaster


00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.763
As extraordinary as aids was


00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.932
In the last quarter
of the last century,


00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:08.101
You should be so lucky
as to be in a community


00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:11.137
Like the queer community
of san francisco.


00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:17.210
- When i talk to young people,
particularly,


00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.945
They'll say,
"what was it like?"


00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:22.415
I mean, the only thing
i can liken it to is a war zone,


00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:25.018
But most of us have never
lived in a war zone.


00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:26.319
But it was...


00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:30.657
you never knew where the bomb
was gonna drop.


00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:36.296
I decided to do
this interview because...


00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:40.633
i've...i've been around
for the entire epidemic,


00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:42.802
And i've seen
so many parts of it,


00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:47.574
And i think there's
a lot of people from--


00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:50.176
I mean, none of my friends
are around.


00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:51.478
From the beginning.


00:01:51.478 --> 00:01:54.080
So i want
to tell their story


00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:57.117
As much as i want
to tell my story.


00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:59.285
I think that's why.


00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:19.672
- I came to san francisco


00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:23.576
Back in the late '70s.


00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:26.179
You know, there were more
gay people coming here.


00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:28.348
There was
all these love children.


00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:31.818
It was right at the end
of the hippies, you know,


00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:35.288
And everybody, i mean,
if you had a bus ticket,


00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:38.324
It better be saying
"san francisco," you know,


00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.927
Because that was
the place to come.


00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.530
I was the dancer.


00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:47.867
I thought i could dance better
than anybody on the west coast.


00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:49.602
Center stage,
i would get up there,


00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:51.337
I'd climb up on that stage,


00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.506
And i'd dance myself
into a frenzy.


00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:56.109
Every sunday night
at the tea dance.


00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:57.844
And if you got too close,


00:02:57.844 --> 00:02:59.579
You might slip off the stage,


00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:03.483
Because you were
too close to me.


00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:07.387
But i thought
i had it going on.


00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:12.592
My dad said one day
that i should sell flowers.


00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:14.327
That's a good business.


00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:18.231
And i thought, "i'm gonna
sell flowers in san francisco,"


00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:19.966
Because, you know,
they've got these songs,


00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.135
Where have all
the flowers gone?


00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.304
And "if you're going
to san francisco,


00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:26.472
Wear a flower
in your hair," and...


00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:29.943
so i was ready for it.


00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:32.545
A friend of mine
came up in a pickup


00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:34.714
And took me right over
into the castro


00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:39.052
On 15th and noe, and i've
been there for 28 years.


00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:42.956
"Hey, i'm one of
the family members," you know.


00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:44.257
"Come buy my flowers."


00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:47.293
So i would put up
these rainbow flags,


00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:50.330
And i--You know, and you could
see 'em from a block away.


00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:51.197
If you looked
down the street.


00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:54.667
You could just see
that little ribbon.


00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.704
Until all the colors faded.


00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:04.210
- I always knew i was gonna
come out to the bay area.


00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.379
And i think a lot of us
came out here


00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:09.849
Because we didn't quite fit
where we were.


00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:15.054
Back in college, i helped start
the first woman's newspaper.


00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:18.958
Uh, we started the first
childcare center.


00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:21.561
Stuff like that.
So i was very involved.


00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:25.031
We had a women's center
on haight street,


00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:26.766
So i started going
to the women's center,


00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.067
And we sat around and said,


00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.236
"Let's open up
a women's clinic,"


00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.537
And then we just did it.


00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:35.875
It was the era
of illegal abortions.


00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:38.478
It was a time
when we, as women,


00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:42.382
Weren't as educated
about our body.


00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:44.117
I was getting
a little older,


00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:45.418
My late--Later 20s,


00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:47.153
And i thought, "eileen,


00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:50.623
You might want
a real job sometime,"


00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:52.792
So i thought,
"i'll just go to nursing school


00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:55.395
And see how i feel about it."


00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:57.997
And, uh, i loved it.


00:04:57.997 --> 00:05:01.467
I loved bedside nursing.


00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:04.937
Once i started working
in the hospital,


00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:06.239
There were all these gay men,


00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:07.540
And it was really fun,


00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.143
'Cause we'd go
clubbing together.


00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:13.179
To the i-Beam,
to the stud.


00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:15.348
You know,
places like that.


00:05:15.348 --> 00:05:20.119
I'd dance and go home
and go to sleep.


00:05:20.553 --> 00:05:22.722
So, you know,
we had a good time.


00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:24.023
It was, like, really fun.


00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:28.795
Unfortunately, none of
those guys are alive today.


00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:34.000
- You know, it's the--The end
of the hippy era in america,


00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.602
And i was a queer kid who--
Who was different,


00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:37.904
Didn't really know
what to do,


00:05:37.904 --> 00:05:40.073
And basically left
buffalo, new york,


00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:41.808
And hitchhiked
around the country


00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:44.844
For a number of years with
the guy i was sleeping with,


00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:47.447
And deliberately
tried to be free,


00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:49.182
It was our--
Sort of our goal,


00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:52.218
And i remember
at one point thinking,


00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:54.387
"Well, i've got nothing but
the backpack and my boyfriend."


00:05:54.821 --> 00:05:56.989
And we literally
actually had nothing.


00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:58.725
"I guess we must be free."


00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:00.893
And it was that
sort of mentality


00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:03.496
That we were pursuing.


00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:05.665
A phrase that
i've sort of come to like


00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:06.532
Is "crazy dreamers,"


00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:07.400
And i was--At that time,


00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:09.135
I thought san francisco
and california


00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:10.436
Was full of crazy dreamers,


00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:13.906
And that was where
i wanted to be.


00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:16.075
I belonged to a little commune


00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:18.678
Of leftover '60s folks,


00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:21.714
Who were trying to establish
an alternative lifestyle,


00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:23.449
And i was struggling with
was i gay,


00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:26.919
Was i bisexual, you know,
what is going on.


00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:28.654
So i come out of the closet,


00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:31.257
In this terrifying moment of
coming to the gay student union


00:06:31.257 --> 00:06:34.727
In san jose state
in september of 1975,


00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:36.896
And the minute
i walked through the door,


00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:39.499
And i'm sure most gay men
of my generation,


00:06:39.499 --> 00:06:41.667
Most queer people are gonna
have a similar experience.


00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:42.535
It was like you're home.


00:06:42.535 --> 00:06:43.836
It's, like,
it all felt familiar.


00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.439
It all seemed like,
"oh.


00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:49.041
How did i not realize this
is where i was supposed to be?"


00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:55.548
- My father really wanted me
to get a master's degree,


00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:57.283
And i really didn't care,


00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:01.187
So the compromise was i would go
to san francisco state,


00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:04.223
'Cause san francisco
was where i wanted to be.


00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:07.693
I liked the people here.
They just seemed more open.


00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:12.899
And i always wanted to meet
a nice blond surfer.


00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:14.200
When i moved
out to california,


00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.501
I was still in the closet.


00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:18.104
I didn't come out of
the closet until after college.


00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:20.273
Um, i came out with a bang.


00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:23.743
I was in a production
ofthe boys in the band.


00:07:25.912 --> 00:07:28.948
For quite a few years,
i was a bit of a workaholic.


00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:32.418
I was in my studio
all the time.


00:07:32.418 --> 00:07:33.719
By the time i was 27,


00:07:33.719 --> 00:07:35.455
I was having one-Man shows
in new york,


00:07:35.888 --> 00:07:37.190
At galleries--
Good galleries in new york.


00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:39.792
And i didn't know
it was supposed to be that easy.


00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:41.093
It was just easy.


00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:44.130
And i was pretty obsessed
with my work,


00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.299
And i was
for quite a long time.


00:07:46.299 --> 00:07:50.203
And--Until i got sick,
really.


00:07:50.203 --> 00:07:52.371
I was first
living in the haight,


00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:55.408
And i remember
walking down haight street,


00:07:55.408 --> 00:07:58.010
And there was this guy handing
out leaflets on the corner,


00:07:58.444 --> 00:07:59.745
And it was harvey.


00:07:59.745 --> 00:08:01.047
It was his first campaign,


00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:02.348
First time he was running,


00:08:02.348 --> 00:08:04.083
And he introduced himself
and i talked to him,


00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:05.384
So i went to work for him.


00:08:05.818 --> 00:08:07.119
And i was handing out
leaflets.


00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:09.722
And, you know, door hangers
and things like that.


00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:11.023
And that was very exciting,


00:08:11.457 --> 00:08:14.927
'Cause i had been somewhat
political in college.


00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:17.530
I'd gotten sick of it,


00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:19.699
Because all my roommates
were s.D.S.,


00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:21.434
And it was very militant.


00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:24.470
And harvey was just
a lot gentler,


00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.507
And a lot more fun.


00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.109
My partner at that time,
steve,


00:08:30.109 --> 00:08:31.844
Was also fairly political.


00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:34.881
Any time there was a march
or a demonstration


00:08:34.881 --> 00:08:37.483
Or a candlelight thing,
we were always there.


00:08:37.917 --> 00:08:39.652
Um, it was important to us.


00:08:40.086 --> 00:08:41.821
Those were the things


00:08:41.821 --> 00:08:44.857
That made us feel connected
to the community.


00:09:08.281 --> 00:09:11.751
Castro street was just
starting to happen,


00:09:11.751 --> 00:09:13.920
And you would always
run into people you knew,


00:09:14.353 --> 00:09:16.522
And it really felt
like a village,


00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:19.125
And the castro
just started to feel


00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:21.727
Like the village
you always wanted.


00:09:31.704 --> 00:09:32.572
- If you took
a bunch of young men


00:09:33.005 --> 00:09:35.174
And said, "have as much sex
as you can have,"


00:09:35.174 --> 00:09:37.343
How much sex
would they have?


00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:39.512
A lot of sex.


00:09:49.488 --> 00:09:53.826
The sense was if gay is good,
gay sex is good, you know?


00:09:53.826 --> 00:09:55.561
And more gay sex
is even better.


00:09:55.561 --> 00:09:59.031
And people often say of--
Of my generation,


00:09:59.031 --> 00:10:01.634
We came to san francisco
to be gay.


00:10:17.249 --> 00:10:20.286
- I remember, like,
january, 1977,


00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.888
I went right down
to castro street.


00:10:22.888 --> 00:10:25.491
Here i've lived in greenwich
village all these years.


00:10:25.925 --> 00:10:27.660
"This--This
is gonna be amazing."


00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:29.395
And i went down,
and, yeah, as you know,


00:10:29.395 --> 00:10:30.696
It's, like, one block long,


00:10:30.696 --> 00:10:32.865
And, like, a block
in either direction.


00:10:33.299 --> 00:10:35.034
And, like, there were
a lot of gay men.


00:10:35.468 --> 00:10:37.637
And, as with
any group of people,


00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:42.408
It was already pretty quickly
falling into little cliques.


00:10:42.408 --> 00:10:45.011
You know, there was, like,
this kind of military look,


00:10:45.011 --> 00:10:47.179
And the kind of the--


00:10:47.179 --> 00:10:48.481
The outdoorsmen look,


00:10:48.914 --> 00:10:50.216
And there was a preppy look,


00:10:50.216 --> 00:10:53.252
And there was already this,
like, kind of western look,


00:10:53.686 --> 00:10:55.421
And a leather look.


00:10:55.421 --> 00:10:57.156
It was already
starting to happen.


00:10:57.156 --> 00:11:00.626
People quickly identifying
as certain male images,


00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:03.229
And i, you know,
i just didn't, like, fit in.


00:11:03.663 --> 00:11:05.398
There wasn't, like,
a long-Haired,


00:11:05.398 --> 00:11:08.868
High-Voiced basketball look,
or something, you know?


00:11:08.868 --> 00:11:11.037
I was just kind of me.


00:11:11.037 --> 00:11:13.205
I mean, i tried.


00:11:13.205 --> 00:11:15.808
I would go and pick up guys
and bring them home,


00:11:15.808 --> 00:11:17.109
And, like,
they would want to go


00:11:17.109 --> 00:11:20.146
From zero to 60 so fast.


00:11:20.146 --> 00:11:21.881
I couldn't do it.


00:11:21.881 --> 00:11:24.917
I was terrible
at anonymous sex.


00:11:25.351 --> 00:11:28.387
I didn't know how to go,
like...


00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:32.291
"all right."


00:11:32.291 --> 00:11:33.592
You know, i just--
I couldn't do it.


00:11:34.026 --> 00:11:37.063
I was like, "hi, my name's ed.
Who are you?"


00:11:37.063 --> 00:11:38.364
You know, and it just--


00:11:38.364 --> 00:11:41.400
It didn't...
it didn't click.


00:11:44.437 --> 00:11:46.172
- I tend to be somebody
who has a partner


00:11:46.605 --> 00:11:47.907
Almost my whole life,


00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:50.943
But i've always been
in open relationships,


00:11:50.943 --> 00:11:55.281
So my sexual outlet
was always the bath houses,


00:11:55.281 --> 00:11:58.317
And they were there,
and they were fun.


00:11:58.317 --> 00:12:00.052
And i would go
with my friends.


00:12:00.052 --> 00:12:02.221
It wasn't, like,
something i would sneak out


00:12:02.655 --> 00:12:03.522
And go on my own.


00:12:03.522 --> 00:12:04.824
It was--It was something
of an outing.


00:12:05.257 --> 00:12:06.992
We would go with friends.


00:12:06.992 --> 00:12:09.595
I remember coming out
of one bath house,


00:12:09.595 --> 00:12:10.896
At, like,
3:00 in the morning,


00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.499
And walking home across the city
in the middle of the night,


00:12:13.499 --> 00:12:14.366
And just thinking,


00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:16.969
"Gee, if my mother
could see me now,


00:12:17.403 --> 00:12:18.270
She'd be just shocked,"


00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.307
But it just felt so good.


00:12:21.307 --> 00:12:22.174
It was, like, a club,


00:12:22.608 --> 00:12:24.343
And we--We called it church.


00:12:24.343 --> 00:12:27.379
It was going to church.


00:12:27.379 --> 00:12:30.416
- Part of it,
you're having sex to have fun.


00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:32.151
Part of it,
you're having sex to find love.


00:12:32.585 --> 00:12:35.187
Part of it,
you're having sex to, uh...


00:12:35.187 --> 00:12:38.657
to--To rebel
against the people


00:12:38.657 --> 00:12:40.392
Who said
you couldn't have sex.


00:12:40.392 --> 00:12:44.296
All of america
was feeling very confident


00:12:44.296 --> 00:12:46.465
That you could be
much more sexual,


00:12:46.465 --> 00:12:48.200
And that was okay.


00:12:48.634 --> 00:12:51.237
Venereal diseases
and unwanted pregnancies,


00:12:51.237 --> 00:12:53.405
It's all curable
with a shot or a pill


00:12:53.405 --> 00:12:55.574
Or something to that effect.


00:13:08.154 --> 00:13:09.889
It's may of 1979,


00:13:10.322 --> 00:13:11.624
And the verdict has come down,


00:13:12.057 --> 00:13:13.793
A verdict on dan white
for the murder of harvey milk,


00:13:13.793 --> 00:13:16.395
And we're all at city hall
protesting.


00:13:16.395 --> 00:13:18.564
There's this enormous rage.


00:13:18.564 --> 00:13:19.865
Thousands of people arrive.


00:13:20.299 --> 00:13:21.167
The police attack.


00:13:21.167 --> 00:13:22.902
We're tear-Gassed,
we're beaten.


00:13:22.902 --> 00:13:25.070
Police cars are burned.


00:13:25.504 --> 00:13:26.806
So this is not a community


00:13:26.806 --> 00:13:29.842
That's feeling really good about
the political establishment


00:13:29.842 --> 00:13:32.444
Going into the 1980s.


00:13:32.444 --> 00:13:35.481
The next night is harvey's
birthday party,


00:13:35.481 --> 00:13:38.083
And so the streets
close off,


00:13:38.517 --> 00:13:39.819
Tens of thousands
of people show up,


00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:42.421
And they give very,
very angry speeches.


00:13:42.421 --> 00:13:45.891
Anne kronenberg
gives a very fierce speech,


00:13:45.891 --> 00:13:46.759
And at the end
of her speech,


00:13:47.193 --> 00:13:48.928
She starts a chant,
"welcome to the '80s,


00:13:49.361 --> 00:13:50.663
Welcome to the '80s."


00:13:51.096 --> 00:13:52.398
We couldn't know,
of course,


00:13:52.398 --> 00:13:55.868
That even then
hiv was present.


00:13:55.868 --> 00:13:58.470
Hiv arrived first
in san francisco


00:13:58.904 --> 00:14:00.206
Probably in '76,


00:14:00.639 --> 00:14:04.109
And by 1979,
probably 10%


00:14:04.109 --> 00:14:05.845
Of the gay men
in that crowd were infected.


00:14:05.845 --> 00:14:07.580
And by the time we discover


00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:08.881
That there is such a thing,


00:14:09.315 --> 00:14:11.050
Aids is even happening,
in june of '81,


00:14:11.483 --> 00:14:13.652
Roughly 20% are infected.


00:14:13.652 --> 00:14:15.387
By the time we actually
get the test,


00:14:15.387 --> 00:14:17.122
So people can find out
if they're infected,


00:14:17.122 --> 00:14:19.291
Close to 50% of the gay men
of san francisco


00:14:19.291 --> 00:14:23.195
Are already infected.


00:15:01.367 --> 00:15:03.535
- '81 was a big year.


00:15:03.535 --> 00:15:06.138
I landed a really good job,


00:15:06.572 --> 00:15:07.873
And, for the first time,


00:15:08.307 --> 00:15:10.042
I was part
of a large office staff


00:15:10.476 --> 00:15:12.645
With a lot of other gay men.


00:15:12.645 --> 00:15:16.115
I was finishing my graduate
degree in creative writing.


00:15:16.115 --> 00:15:17.850
I went to europe.
I had this great job.


00:15:18.284 --> 00:15:21.754
All these new gay men
i was working with.


00:15:21.754 --> 00:15:24.356
And, um, i felt like,


00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:26.525
"Oh, the '80s.
Something's gonna shift."


00:15:26.959 --> 00:15:28.694
Like, i moved
to new york in '71.


00:15:29.128 --> 00:15:33.465
Now i'm really here
in san francisco in '81.


00:15:33.899 --> 00:15:38.237
And--And so that is
when everything changed,


00:15:38.671 --> 00:15:43.876
Because that was, you know,
that was the year in the castro.


00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:46.045
Running down,
i'll never forget,


00:15:46.045 --> 00:15:48.213
I went to
the castro theatre.


00:15:48.647 --> 00:15:49.949
Great double feature.


00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:51.684
Two--I think it was, uh,


00:15:51.684 --> 00:15:54.286
It was, like,
now, voyagerandcasablanca


00:15:54.286 --> 00:15:55.587
On the big screen,


00:15:55.587 --> 00:15:58.190
And i remember, like,
running down to the--


00:15:58.190 --> 00:15:59.925
The old star pharmacy,


00:15:59.925 --> 00:16:01.660
'Cause we're gonna
smoke some pot,


00:16:02.094 --> 00:16:04.263
And we didn't have any papers.


00:16:04.697 --> 00:16:06.865
And i remember
looking in the...


00:16:09.468 --> 00:16:12.938
i remember looking in the window
of star pharmacy,


00:16:12.938 --> 00:16:16.408
And there were these little
polaroid photographs


00:16:16.408 --> 00:16:19.445
That this young man
had made of himself.


00:16:19.445 --> 00:16:21.613
There were at least three,
maybe four of them.


00:16:22.047 --> 00:16:24.216
The first one was like this.


00:16:24.216 --> 00:16:27.252
And inside...


00:16:30.723 --> 00:16:34.626
these big,
purple splotches.


00:16:34.626 --> 00:16:36.362
And then there was
another picture,


00:16:36.795 --> 00:16:38.530
And he had taken his shirt,
and pulled it up like this.


00:16:38.964 --> 00:16:40.699
It was of his chest,


00:16:40.699 --> 00:16:42.868
Big purple splotches.


00:16:43.302 --> 00:16:44.603
They were just on the window,


00:16:44.603 --> 00:16:46.338
And underneath,
there was a hand-Written note


00:16:46.338 --> 00:16:48.073
That said something like,
"watch out, guys.


00:16:48.073 --> 00:16:49.375
There's something out there."


00:16:49.375 --> 00:16:52.411
Something like that.


00:16:52.845 --> 00:16:54.580
And, uh, oh, my god.


00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:59.785
It made...
huge impact on me.


00:16:59.785 --> 00:17:01.520
And then, like,
i was really stoned,


00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:03.255
And i went and watched
the movie, and...


00:17:03.255 --> 00:17:04.990
but the whole movie


00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:08.027
I was just
thinking about that.


00:17:08.027 --> 00:17:11.063
It really made
an impact on me.


00:17:11.063 --> 00:17:16.268
I went to see the movies with
a friend of mine named michael,


00:17:16.268 --> 00:17:18.003
And he and i
worked together,


00:17:18.437 --> 00:17:20.172
And he had woken up
kind of recently


00:17:20.172 --> 00:17:25.811
With this, like,
red splotch in his eye.


00:17:25.811 --> 00:17:29.281
And he kept going, like,
"what is this? What is this?"


00:17:29.715 --> 00:17:33.619
And, um, he, um, he had been
going to the eye doctor,


00:17:33.619 --> 00:17:36.655
And they hadn't been able
to figure out what it was,


00:17:36.655 --> 00:17:40.125
And...
[sighs]


00:17:40.125 --> 00:17:42.728
You know, i...


00:17:42.728 --> 00:17:44.029
it turned out to be ks.


00:17:44.029 --> 00:17:46.198
He had ks in his eye.


00:17:46.198 --> 00:17:48.801
So it was right there
in the movie line with us.


00:17:48.801 --> 00:17:53.572
Like, already.
Like, it was already there.


00:17:57.042 --> 00:17:58.343
- The pictures
show the progression


00:17:58.343 --> 00:18:00.079
Of how a few red bumps


00:18:00.512 --> 00:18:01.814
Turn into the mark
of kaposi's sarcoma.


00:18:01.814 --> 00:18:04.416
It's a rare cancer normally
found in the elderly,


00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:06.151
But now
it's striking young men,


00:18:06.585 --> 00:18:08.320
Most of whom are gay,
like bobbi campbell.


00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:10.923
After one month,
tests are still being done


00:18:10.923 --> 00:18:12.658
On the red bumps
on his foot.


00:18:13.092 --> 00:18:14.393
- I don't know
how i got it.


00:18:14.393 --> 00:18:17.429
I fit the profile of kind of
a typical kaposi's patient


00:18:17.863 --> 00:18:20.466
In my age,
and that i'm gay, and...


00:18:22.634 --> 00:18:25.671
but i don't know
how i got it.


00:18:25.671 --> 00:18:27.406
- The first time
i heard about aids,


00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:29.575
I think it was called
the gay cancer.


00:18:29.575 --> 00:18:31.743
It was ks.


00:18:32.177 --> 00:18:33.479
It was terrifying.


00:18:33.912 --> 00:18:36.949
And we had friends
who were dying


00:18:36.949 --> 00:18:39.118
Right at the beginning
of the epidemic.


00:18:39.551 --> 00:18:44.323
I mean, this one person
who helped my career greatly,


00:18:44.756 --> 00:18:47.793
Who was a curator
of the brooklyn museum,


00:18:47.793 --> 00:18:49.962
Gave me a show
at the brooklyn museum,


00:18:49.962 --> 00:18:52.564
And he died before
the show happened,


00:18:52.998 --> 00:18:55.601
And that was--
We--Now looking back,


00:18:55.601 --> 00:18:56.902
I know he died of aids,


00:18:56.902 --> 00:18:59.505
But back then
there was no name for it.


00:19:02.541 --> 00:19:05.577
- I was hanging blood
one day in the hospital,


00:19:05.577 --> 00:19:07.746
And this was, you know,


00:19:07.746 --> 00:19:09.915
Before the times
that you wore gloves.


00:19:09.915 --> 00:19:12.518
And the infectious
disease fellow


00:19:12.518 --> 00:19:15.120
Came in and said, "eileen,
why don't you put gloves on?


00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:17.723
We don't know what this is."


00:19:21.627 --> 00:19:24.229
- I was selling flowers
at that time,


00:19:24.229 --> 00:19:27.266
And there was a guy
down the street.


00:19:27.699 --> 00:19:29.868
Five days.


00:19:30.302 --> 00:19:32.905
One day he went
to the hospital,


00:19:32.905 --> 00:19:35.941
Five days later,
he was dead.


00:19:37.676 --> 00:19:39.845
- I'm looking through
the gay periodicals,


00:19:39.845 --> 00:19:44.183
And in one of them,
new cancer described.


00:19:44.616 --> 00:19:45.918
And so i'm aware
something has occurred.


00:19:45.918 --> 00:19:47.219
And i noted--


00:19:47.219 --> 00:19:50.689
I think everybody who was paying
attention to the community


00:19:50.689 --> 00:19:54.593
Noted, "well, this could be
something to pay attention to,"


00:19:54.593 --> 00:19:56.762
And so we--I did.


00:20:06.305 --> 00:20:09.775
- People were coming in
with pneumocystis pneumonia,


00:20:10.209 --> 00:20:13.245
Who were quite well,
you know, one day.


00:20:13.245 --> 00:20:18.450
You know, uh, out there
swimming, playing tennis.


00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:20.185
You know, buffed.


00:20:20.185 --> 00:20:22.788
Coming in and--
Were dying.


00:20:22.788 --> 00:20:26.258
I mean,
were dead ten days later.


00:20:26.692 --> 00:20:29.728
People would come in
with kaposi's sarcoma.


00:20:29.728 --> 00:20:32.764
There might be one little legion
or two little legions,


00:20:32.764 --> 00:20:34.499
And they would grow.


00:20:34.499 --> 00:20:38.403
And maybe a legion would cut off
circulation in their leg,


00:20:38.403 --> 00:20:40.572
And their leg
would balloon up,


00:20:40.572 --> 00:20:42.307
Or maybe it would
get into their lung,


00:20:42.307 --> 00:20:44.042
And they couldn't breathe.


00:20:44.042 --> 00:20:48.814
And maybe they would
just waste away.


00:20:48.814 --> 00:20:53.151
- Very early, certainly within
the first 18 months,


00:20:53.151 --> 00:20:59.658
I assumed that a number of my
friends were likely infected,


00:20:59.658 --> 00:21:02.261
And probably myself and--


00:21:02.261 --> 00:21:05.297
And all the people
in my group were infected.


00:21:09.201 --> 00:21:12.237
[Dramatic music]


00:21:12.237 --> 00:21:19.177
♪ ♪


00:21:44.336 --> 00:21:46.071
- From the beginning,


00:21:46.071 --> 00:21:48.674
I just couldn't stand
the homophobia


00:21:49.107 --> 00:21:52.577
And the prejudice
that was going on,


00:21:52.577 --> 00:21:53.445
And the fear.


00:21:53.445 --> 00:21:55.614
There was incredible fear,
right?


00:21:55.614 --> 00:21:57.349
These people
were coming in and dying,


00:21:57.349 --> 00:21:59.084
And nobody knew what it was,


00:21:59.084 --> 00:22:02.120
And people get afraid.


00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:04.723
There were people who were
afraid to go into rooms,


00:22:05.157 --> 00:22:09.494
And so i found myself
going into the rooms.


00:22:09.494 --> 00:22:13.398
If you're not a family member,
they wouldn't talk to you,


00:22:13.398 --> 00:22:16.001
So if somebody's partner
was in there,


00:22:16.001 --> 00:22:19.905
The doctors might not explain
to them what was going on.


00:22:19.905 --> 00:22:24.676
So i found myself
talking to them.


00:22:24.676 --> 00:22:27.713
It was a weird time
in the hospital,


00:22:28.146 --> 00:22:30.315
Because they didn't want
to be associated


00:22:30.749 --> 00:22:32.050
As an "aids hospital,"


00:22:32.484 --> 00:22:35.087
Because no one would want
to come to the hospital


00:22:35.087 --> 00:22:37.689
If they knew we were
an "aids hospital."


00:22:37.689 --> 00:22:41.159
So there was a lot
of struggle there.


00:22:42.461 --> 00:22:44.629
I remember my mom.


00:22:44.629 --> 00:22:48.967
She was saying,
"why do you have to do this?"


00:22:49.401 --> 00:22:50.702
You know,
'cause i've already put my mom


00:22:51.136 --> 00:22:52.003
Through lots of stuff.


00:22:52.437 --> 00:22:53.739
And i remember saying to her,


00:22:53.739 --> 00:22:56.775
"Mom, it didn't choose--


00:22:56.775 --> 00:22:59.811
I didn't choose it.
It chose me."


00:23:00.245 --> 00:23:01.113
'Cause you're there,


00:23:01.113 --> 00:23:03.281
And this terrible thing
is happening,


00:23:03.281 --> 00:23:05.016
And you're a nurse,
and you can help,


00:23:05.016 --> 00:23:08.487
And sometimes that's just
helping somebody die,


00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:14.126
But i, you know,
i couldn't turn my back to it.


00:23:22.367 --> 00:23:24.536
- Something was happening.


00:23:24.536 --> 00:23:26.271
That these gay men
were showing up


00:23:26.705 --> 00:23:29.741
At places like united way,


00:23:29.741 --> 00:23:31.476
Looking for a support group,


00:23:31.476 --> 00:23:34.513
Or, um...


00:23:34.513 --> 00:23:37.549
uh...social services.


00:23:37.983 --> 00:23:39.718
Because they had no...


00:23:39.718 --> 00:23:42.754
they had no family.


00:23:43.188 --> 00:23:46.224
I saw an ad
in thebay area reporter.


00:23:46.224 --> 00:23:48.393
Shanti project
was looking for people


00:23:48.827 --> 00:23:52.731
Who'd be willing to be a buddy
to someone with this illness.


00:23:52.731 --> 00:23:57.068
And i took the second
shanti volunteer training


00:23:57.502 --> 00:23:59.237
That occurred here
in san francisco,


00:23:59.671 --> 00:24:03.575
And i got matched
with someone immediately.


00:24:03.575 --> 00:24:05.744
I hadn't met a person
with aids yet


00:24:05.744 --> 00:24:10.081
Who was just kind of, like,
off on his own, and...


00:24:10.081 --> 00:24:13.985
like, expecting...


00:24:13.985 --> 00:24:18.323
that someone was gonna come,
and, like, help him.


00:24:18.323 --> 00:24:20.926
And, um...


00:24:24.396 --> 00:24:28.300
i just remember
going to his apartment, and...


00:24:28.733 --> 00:24:32.637
just him opening the door,
and...


00:24:32.637 --> 00:24:34.806
he said his name was ed.


00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:40.445
I said
my name was ed too.


00:24:40.445 --> 00:24:43.048
And, you know, like,
lo and behold,


00:24:43.482 --> 00:24:46.084
My way
of being with gay men


00:24:46.518 --> 00:24:49.120
Suddenly...


00:24:49.554 --> 00:24:52.157
was perfect.


00:24:52.157 --> 00:24:55.627
Like, "hi."


00:24:55.627 --> 00:25:02.567
Like, "who are you?
How are you doing?"


00:25:02.567 --> 00:25:06.471
I took my training
in july of '83,


00:25:06.471 --> 00:25:09.508
And of course i was close
to all these gay men.


00:25:09.508 --> 00:25:11.676
There were seven gay men
working in this office,


00:25:11.676 --> 00:25:14.279
And i was coming in
and telling them, like,


00:25:14.279 --> 00:25:15.580
"Oh, my god..."


00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:19.050
And, you know, they think
it's transmitted sexually,


00:25:19.484 --> 00:25:22.954
And they're thinking condoms
is a way to protect us,


00:25:22.954 --> 00:25:25.991
And they're telling us
don't use poppers.


00:25:25.991 --> 00:25:29.027
And i go, "it's already, like,
disseminating information."


00:25:29.027 --> 00:25:31.196
Back then, especially,


00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:35.534
There was this
whole dynamic about


00:25:35.534 --> 00:25:39.004
How are you getting it?
Who are you getting it from?


00:25:39.004 --> 00:25:40.739
Who's giving it to who?


00:25:41.172 --> 00:25:43.775
Um, in that little office,


00:25:43.775 --> 00:25:45.076
Some of that feeling, like,


00:25:45.076 --> 00:25:47.679
I'm pretty sure they had
all had sex with one another.


00:25:48.113 --> 00:25:49.848
But, once again,
in my kind of mismatched way,


00:25:50.282 --> 00:25:52.884
I hadn't had sex
with any of them.


00:25:52.884 --> 00:25:55.487
Um...


00:25:55.487 --> 00:25:57.656
they all...


00:25:57.656 --> 00:26:01.126
they all got infected.


00:26:01.126 --> 00:26:03.728
And they all died
except one.


00:26:08.934 --> 00:26:13.705
- My partner, steve,
was an immunology researcher.


00:26:13.705 --> 00:26:15.874
We'd been together
for quite a while,


00:26:15.874 --> 00:26:18.043
Probably about eight years.


00:26:18.043 --> 00:26:19.344
And, all of a sudden,


00:26:19.344 --> 00:26:21.079
People were coming to him


00:26:21.079 --> 00:26:23.682
And asking him
to explain what's going on,


00:26:24.115 --> 00:26:25.417
And it was interesting.


00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:28.019
I mean, his self esteem
sort of turned around,


00:26:28.019 --> 00:26:32.357
Because he was a holder
of very important information.


00:26:32.357 --> 00:26:35.827
He ended up working
in jay levy's lab,


00:26:35.827 --> 00:26:37.996
Which was one
of the most important


00:26:37.996 --> 00:26:40.599
Aids research labs
in the world.


00:26:40.599 --> 00:26:44.069
We got tested because
steve took my blood


00:26:44.502 --> 00:26:45.804
And brought it
into jay levy's lab,


00:26:46.237 --> 00:26:47.539
So we were, like,
some of the first people


00:26:47.973 --> 00:26:49.708
Who knew that we were
actually positive,


00:26:50.141 --> 00:26:53.612
'Cause the test
wasn't even available.


00:26:53.612 --> 00:26:57.515
When steve came back
from jay levy's lab


00:26:57.949 --> 00:27:03.154
And told me that we were
both hiv positive,


00:27:03.154 --> 00:27:06.625
It--My life
changed completely.


00:27:07.058 --> 00:27:11.396
Um, i--I had had five people
working for me,


00:27:11.396 --> 00:27:16.601
Um, and i let them go,


00:27:16.601 --> 00:27:19.204
And, luckily,
i had saved some money,


00:27:19.638 --> 00:27:22.240
And i just
started doing sculpture.


00:27:26.578 --> 00:27:28.747
- Here am i,
the kid from san jose,


00:27:28.747 --> 00:27:30.482
I'm up here,
i'm now the vice president


00:27:30.482 --> 00:27:31.783
Of some little
gay democratic club


00:27:32.217 --> 00:27:34.819
Where maybe 15 or 20
people show up,


00:27:35.253 --> 00:27:37.856
And suddenly the community
starts to die


00:27:37.856 --> 00:27:40.025
Of these extraordinary,
horrible diseases,


00:27:40.458 --> 00:27:42.193
And they want help.


00:27:42.193 --> 00:27:44.796
"How do we--" You know,
"how do we keep 'em alive?


00:27:44.796 --> 00:27:46.531
"How do we make sure
they don't die of starvation


00:27:46.531 --> 00:27:48.700
Because they can't cook?
How do we--"


00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:51.302
Meanwhile, there's all these
attacks that are occurring.


00:27:51.736 --> 00:27:53.038
Meanwhile, there's
this tremendous debate


00:27:53.471 --> 00:27:54.339
Within the community about--


00:27:54.339 --> 00:27:56.074
"Well, maybe these
are all wrong decisions.


00:27:56.074 --> 00:27:57.375
Maybe we shouldn't
be sexually free."


00:27:57.809 --> 00:28:00.412
Maybe--And all these
other debates are occurring.


00:28:00.412 --> 00:28:03.014
But it's occurring--
The leadership, such as it is,


00:28:03.448 --> 00:28:06.484
Is guys like me,
who are suddenly,


00:28:06.484 --> 00:28:07.786
In this little group,


00:28:07.786 --> 00:28:11.256
Were forced to deal with this
unbelievable circumstance


00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:15.593
Of a community that, in addition
to being hated and under attack,


00:28:16.027 --> 00:28:18.630
Is now forced alone
to try to figure out


00:28:18.630 --> 00:28:23.401
How to deal with this
extraordinary medical disaster.


00:28:23.401 --> 00:28:27.305
People would see
my picture in theb.A.R.


00:28:27.305 --> 00:28:30.341
And come up to me and say,
"i was diagnosed. What do i do?


00:28:30.775 --> 00:28:32.510
"Do you know a doctor?
What do i do?


00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:34.679
"Is it true, you know,
this might occur?


00:28:34.679 --> 00:28:35.980
What do i do?"


00:28:36.414 --> 00:28:39.017
We held a series
of town hall meetings,


00:28:39.017 --> 00:28:42.053
And a group called mobilization
against aids was created.


00:28:42.053 --> 00:28:44.222
And i was their first e.D.,


00:28:44.222 --> 00:28:48.560
And that's sort of how
i formally enter into aids work.


00:28:48.993 --> 00:28:52.030
Mobilization's purpose
was to demand a greater response


00:28:52.030 --> 00:28:54.632
To the hiv aids pandemic.


00:28:54.632 --> 00:28:57.669
The first response was to try
to take care of the sick.


00:28:57.669 --> 00:28:58.970
That's the first response.


00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:01.573
The second response
was to try to stop people,


00:29:01.573 --> 00:29:03.742
Um, from getting infected.


00:29:03.742 --> 00:29:07.645
The third response was--
How do we advocate?


00:29:07.645 --> 00:29:08.947
How do we now
get other people involved


00:29:09.380 --> 00:29:11.116
To be able
to generate resources?


00:29:11.116 --> 00:29:14.586
We are here to try to spark
across the land


00:29:14.586 --> 00:29:18.056
General citizen support for
the actions that are being led


00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:20.225
So overwhelmingly
by people with aids,


00:29:20.658 --> 00:29:22.827
To try to get
the nation to move


00:29:22.827 --> 00:29:25.430
Into an effective response
of this epidemic.


00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:28.900
We lead a delegation of people
with aids to washington.


00:29:29.334 --> 00:29:31.069
Here's guys, very sick,


00:29:31.069 --> 00:29:32.804
They're in end sta--
By definition,


00:29:32.804 --> 00:29:34.105
They're in end-Stage aids.


00:29:34.539 --> 00:29:37.142
There is no treatments to--
To speak of.


00:29:37.142 --> 00:29:38.877
Maybe there's some
experimental treatments


00:29:38.877 --> 00:29:40.178
They're starting to get.


00:29:40.178 --> 00:29:41.913
And here they are,
flying on planes,


00:29:41.913 --> 00:29:43.648
Going across the country
with no money,


00:29:43.648 --> 00:29:45.383
Sleeping four to a room


00:29:45.817 --> 00:29:47.986
To be able to go do opping.


00:29:47.986 --> 00:29:49.287
And my experience is,


00:29:49.721 --> 00:29:52.323
My belief is, all those folks
thought they would die.


00:29:52.757 --> 00:29:54.492
None of them thought
they would survive aids.


00:29:54.492 --> 00:29:56.227
They were doing it
because they thought


00:29:56.661 --> 00:29:57.962
In so doing they would make it


00:29:58.396 --> 00:30:00.565
So other people
from the community and beyond


00:30:00.565 --> 00:30:02.300
Were able to live.


00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:04.903
And that happened
many, many, many times,


00:30:04.903 --> 00:30:08.373
Where people with aids would
just do extraordinary things.


00:30:08.373 --> 00:30:12.710
That's who was, in fact,
leading the response.


00:30:15.747 --> 00:30:19.217
- When he went to the hospital,
i followed him there.


00:30:19.217 --> 00:30:21.386
So i went to 5-B,


00:30:21.386 --> 00:30:24.422
Which was right here at
san francisco general hospital,


00:30:24.422 --> 00:30:27.892
To--To visit him,
as a shanti volunteer.


00:30:28.326 --> 00:30:32.230
And 5-B was a seven-Bed unit,


00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:35.266
An old intensive care unit
that had been turned into


00:30:35.266 --> 00:30:42.207
The first aids-Dedicated
hospital unit in the world.


00:30:42.207 --> 00:30:47.846
And everybody who worked there
was there on a volunteer basis.


00:30:47.846 --> 00:30:52.183
1983, which they weren't sure
how it was transmitted.


00:30:52.183 --> 00:30:54.352
So they didn't
want anybody working there


00:30:54.352 --> 00:30:56.955
Who was gonna have
contagion issues.


00:30:56.955 --> 00:30:59.991
So they wanted to make sure here
at san francisco general


00:31:00.425 --> 00:31:03.461
That you were not gonna be
coming from that kind of fear.


00:31:03.461 --> 00:31:05.630
You'd be volunteering
to work here.


00:31:06.931 --> 00:31:09.100
This is where
i started encountering,


00:31:09.534 --> 00:31:12.570
Like, lesbians, coming
and working on the aids unit


00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:15.607
With all these gay men
who were dying.


00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:18.209
It was so moving,
because certainly gay men


00:31:18.209 --> 00:31:21.246
Were not making a whole lot
of room for lesbians.


00:31:21.246 --> 00:31:22.981
Let's put it that way.
Back then.


00:31:22.981 --> 00:31:27.318
Um...


00:31:27.318 --> 00:31:33.825
so i got this sense
of this group of people


00:31:34.259 --> 00:31:39.030
Who were really caring
for these men who were dying.


00:31:44.235 --> 00:31:46.404
- Steve became
more and more obsessed


00:31:46.404 --> 00:31:49.007
With trying to find out
what the latest treatments were.


00:31:49.007 --> 00:31:51.175
He wanted to save our lives.


00:31:51.175 --> 00:31:53.344
He wanted to figure out,
you know,


00:31:53.344 --> 00:31:57.248
You know, how we were
gonna beat this thing.


00:31:57.248 --> 00:31:59.851
And he found out
about a study


00:32:00.285 --> 00:32:05.924
That was done in africa
with a drug called suramin.


00:32:05.924 --> 00:32:08.526
And they were doing--


00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:11.996
They were doing the study
here at san francisco general,


00:32:11.996 --> 00:32:15.466
And he got us both
into the study.


00:32:15.466 --> 00:32:17.635
Across the country, there was,
like, three study sites.


00:32:18.069 --> 00:32:19.804
There were, like,
80 people in the study.


00:32:19.804 --> 00:32:24.142
And the drug was hideous.


00:32:24.575 --> 00:32:26.744
It was...
you'd go in,


00:32:27.178 --> 00:32:28.913
And it was, like,
two hours of i.V.,


00:32:28.913 --> 00:32:30.648
And for the next two days,


00:32:30.648 --> 00:32:34.118
You literally felt like
you'd been run over by a truck.


00:32:34.118 --> 00:32:36.721
And i was a wuss.


00:32:37.155 --> 00:32:39.324
And i--I just--
After a month of this,


00:32:39.324 --> 00:32:41.059
I just said,
"i can't take this."


00:32:41.059 --> 00:32:43.661
It's just, you know...


00:32:43.661 --> 00:32:46.698
i was just--
It just made me so sick,


00:32:46.698 --> 00:32:47.999
And i hated it.


00:32:47.999 --> 00:32:49.734
But steve just kept on going,


00:32:49.734 --> 00:32:52.770
And he had had
chronic hepatitis b


00:32:52.770 --> 00:32:55.807
From a needle stick
that he'd gotten in a lab


00:32:56.240 --> 00:32:57.976
When he was working
in a lab,


00:32:58.409 --> 00:33:01.879
And it activated
his hepatitis.


00:33:01.879 --> 00:33:04.482
And within--


00:33:04.482 --> 00:33:07.518
We started, i think,
the study in july.


00:33:07.952 --> 00:33:11.422
He quit the study
in october.


00:33:11.422 --> 00:33:14.025
And he was...


00:33:19.230 --> 00:33:23.134
he was dead by january.


00:33:23.134 --> 00:33:27.905
It was really quick.


00:33:27.905 --> 00:33:29.640
Um...


00:33:30.074 --> 00:33:36.147
and everybody in that study
died except for me.


00:33:36.147 --> 00:33:39.183
'Cause i was a wuss.
I couldn't take it.


00:33:39.183 --> 00:33:43.955
And i'm so glad
i took care of myself that way.


00:33:43.955 --> 00:33:49.594
But i talked to a doctor
in the study afterwards,


00:33:49.594 --> 00:33:53.064
And they had a meeting
of all the doctors


00:33:53.498 --> 00:33:54.365
And people who had--


00:33:54.365 --> 00:33:56.534
Researchers
across the country


00:33:56.534 --> 00:33:59.137
Who had been involved
in the study,


00:33:59.137 --> 00:34:03.041
And he said he never...


00:34:03.041 --> 00:34:08.246
he'd never been in a room
of doctors sobbing before.


00:34:08.246 --> 00:34:12.150
They had lost
all their patients.


00:34:12.150 --> 00:34:14.318
Very quickly.


00:34:14.318 --> 00:34:16.921
So that was one of the first
disasters in aids treatment,


00:34:17.355 --> 00:34:19.524
I think,
that really made everybody


00:34:19.524 --> 00:34:22.560
Really careful after this.


00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:27.331
Um...


00:34:27.331 --> 00:34:30.802
[sighs]


00:34:31.235 --> 00:34:34.705
Steve was 35.


00:34:39.043 --> 00:34:40.778
Two weeks after steve died,


00:34:40.778 --> 00:34:42.513
My best friend died.


00:34:42.513 --> 00:34:46.851
Peter.


00:34:46.851 --> 00:34:48.586
Two days before steve died,


00:34:48.586 --> 00:34:49.887
Another good friend died.


00:34:50.321 --> 00:34:53.791
I mean, it was just--
It was an avalanche.


00:35:41.072 --> 00:35:44.976
- Within a mile of epicenter
of castro and market,


00:35:44.976 --> 00:35:46.711
Large numbers
of people died.


00:35:46.711 --> 00:35:48.012
And not just
your friends who died,


00:35:48.012 --> 00:35:50.181
But, you know,
the people you didn't know.


00:35:50.181 --> 00:35:51.482
The friend of the friend.


00:35:51.482 --> 00:35:53.217
You know,
you'd go get a coffee,


00:35:53.217 --> 00:35:55.820
And the person who used
to give you coffee's died.


00:35:55.820 --> 00:35:57.555
You would, you know,
whatever it was--


00:35:57.555 --> 00:35:59.724
Your banker, your mailman,
all that.


00:35:59.724 --> 00:36:01.459
Mass, mass death.


00:36:01.893 --> 00:36:04.061
To the point where you,
to some degree,


00:36:04.061 --> 00:36:06.230
You would stop asking,
if people weren't around,


00:36:06.230 --> 00:36:08.399
Where they were, unless you
wanted to get into a discussion


00:36:08.399 --> 00:36:11.002
Of them being dead
or them being sick.


00:36:11.435 --> 00:36:14.472
So, for a number of years,


00:36:14.472 --> 00:36:17.942
People are all assuming
we've got this disease,


00:36:18.376 --> 00:36:20.978
And it's very likely
we'll be dead soon.


00:36:24.448 --> 00:36:27.485
- Everybody was reading
the obituaries,


00:36:27.485 --> 00:36:30.955
Because they went from
like this to like this.


00:36:30.955 --> 00:36:33.991
You know, it was just,
like, "oh, my god."


00:36:33.991 --> 00:36:37.028
And everybody would get
theb.A.R.Every week


00:36:37.461 --> 00:36:39.630
Just to see who's gone.


00:36:39.630 --> 00:36:42.667
Being the flower man,


00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:44.835
I was thrown
into the middle of it,


00:36:44.835 --> 00:36:47.872
Because a lot of people
would say,


00:36:47.872 --> 00:36:51.342
"Guy, my friend died,


00:36:51.776 --> 00:36:55.680
"And i don't have enough money
to buy flowers, and...


00:36:55.680 --> 00:37:00.017
i need some help.
Can you help us?"


00:37:00.017 --> 00:37:02.620
They wanted
to bury their friends


00:37:02.620 --> 00:37:06.958
With a lot of dignity
and beauty, and...


00:37:06.958 --> 00:37:10.861
and "i came to you
to help me out."


00:37:13.030 --> 00:37:13.898
You know,
i'm emotional,


00:37:14.332 --> 00:37:16.067
Because this is the first time
i thought about it.


00:37:16.067 --> 00:37:20.838
I--I can't even count the
funerals that i did, you know?


00:37:20.838 --> 00:37:23.007
And if it wasn't
no more than--


00:37:23.441 --> 00:37:26.043
You know, some people
would bring me a vase,


00:37:26.043 --> 00:37:27.778
And they said, "guy,
this is all i can afford.


00:37:27.778 --> 00:37:30.381
Can you put some
flowers in it, or--?"


00:37:30.381 --> 00:37:31.682
You know, and i did that.


00:37:31.682 --> 00:37:34.719
And i, you know,
it was never about money.


00:37:34.719 --> 00:37:36.454
It was about love,
you know.


00:37:36.454 --> 00:37:37.755
It was about these people.


00:37:37.755 --> 00:37:39.924
Not letting my friends down.


00:37:39.924 --> 00:37:43.394
You know, just helping them
to the other side.


00:37:43.828 --> 00:37:46.430
Mm.


00:37:56.841 --> 00:37:58.576
- Today i have ordered
the closure


00:37:58.576 --> 00:38:01.178
Of 14 commercial
establishments


00:38:01.178 --> 00:38:05.516
Which promote and profit
from the spread of aids.


00:38:05.516 --> 00:38:07.685
- There was a broad view


00:38:08.119 --> 00:38:09.854
That there was
a sexual transmission component


00:38:09.854 --> 00:38:11.155
Of the disease.


00:38:11.155 --> 00:38:15.059
So here we are debating
how do we continue to have sex,


00:38:15.493 --> 00:38:17.228
How do we continue
to love each other,


00:38:17.228 --> 00:38:18.963
How do we continue to be--


00:38:18.963 --> 00:38:23.301
To pursue the dream of the
community that we want to have


00:38:23.301 --> 00:38:25.036
In the midst of this plague,


00:38:25.469 --> 00:38:26.771
And so then
comes the discussion,


00:38:27.204 --> 00:38:28.506
Well, the government


00:38:28.506 --> 00:38:31.542
Would like to shut down
some institutions,


00:38:31.542 --> 00:38:35.012
And some of these are old,
core institutions,


00:38:35.446 --> 00:38:36.314
Which is the bath houses.


00:38:36.747 --> 00:38:37.615
There's always
been bath houses.


00:38:38.049 --> 00:38:40.217
They precede the gay community
as we know it,


00:38:40.651 --> 00:38:42.820
Where gay people would go
and meet and have sex,


00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:46.290
And some people thought
that was a good idea,


00:38:46.290 --> 00:38:49.760
The bath houses are run by
irresponsible business owners


00:38:49.760 --> 00:38:51.495
Who are--Just don't care
about the pandemic


00:38:51.495 --> 00:38:52.797
And are ripping people off,


00:38:52.797 --> 00:38:56.267
And other people thought
this is a dangerous precedent.


00:38:56.267 --> 00:38:58.002
That your friend
the government


00:38:58.002 --> 00:38:59.737
Would like to shut down
these institutions.


00:38:59.737 --> 00:39:01.472
Is that--That okay with you?


00:39:01.472 --> 00:39:02.340
The majority
of the community


00:39:02.773 --> 00:39:04.942
Felt that we were in
a crisis right now,


00:39:05.376 --> 00:39:06.677
And the baths
needed to be shut down.


00:39:07.111 --> 00:39:11.015
And a lot of people
were very afraid of it,


00:39:11.015 --> 00:39:13.617
And so the community divided.


00:39:13.617 --> 00:39:14.919
And, to some degree,


00:39:14.919 --> 00:39:16.654
A split also between
the women's community,


00:39:16.654 --> 00:39:18.389
The lesbian community,
and gay men,


00:39:18.389 --> 00:39:23.160
Where gay men kept being
controversial to a degree,


00:39:23.594 --> 00:39:25.329
By insisting
on having as much sex


00:39:25.329 --> 00:39:26.630
In as many places
as they were doing,


00:39:27.064 --> 00:39:28.799
And the women's community


00:39:28.799 --> 00:39:31.836
Was, you know,
to some degree saying,


00:39:31.836 --> 00:39:32.703
You know,
we don't know--


00:39:33.137 --> 00:39:34.004
This is not the commu--


00:39:34.438 --> 00:39:36.173
This is not the core
definitions of the community


00:39:36.173 --> 00:39:38.342
That we think the community
should be fighting over.


00:39:38.342 --> 00:39:40.077
We don't think
the central battles


00:39:40.077 --> 00:39:43.114
Of glbt liberation
should be about, you know,


00:39:43.114 --> 00:39:44.415
Public sex, for example.


00:39:44.849 --> 00:39:47.017
We think there should be
a broader discussion.


00:39:47.017 --> 00:39:51.355
So it was a high,
high tension debate.


00:39:56.127 --> 00:40:00.030
- Since i did sit on the corner
for 28 years,


00:40:00.464 --> 00:40:04.802
I just saw the progression
of people, you know,


00:40:05.236 --> 00:40:08.706
So scary just to,
all of a sudden, you know,


00:40:08.706 --> 00:40:10.441
They'd be walking
down the street,


00:40:10.441 --> 00:40:11.742
And then the next time
you see them,


00:40:11.742 --> 00:40:14.779
They would be
walking with a cane,


00:40:14.779 --> 00:40:16.947
Or they'd be in a wheelchair.


00:40:16.947 --> 00:40:19.116
And that was devastating to--


00:40:19.116 --> 00:40:23.020
"Oh, i remember him."


00:40:26.056 --> 00:40:27.792
- Here's the gay community,


00:40:27.792 --> 00:40:29.960
Which,
for better or for worse,


00:40:30.394 --> 00:40:32.563
Is very concerned
with appearances,


00:40:32.997 --> 00:40:35.166
And here comes this disease
that manifests itself


00:40:35.599 --> 00:40:37.768
And destroys
your physical appearance.


00:40:37.768 --> 00:40:39.069
It's the first thing it does,


00:40:39.069 --> 00:40:41.238
Whether it's ks or wasting.


00:40:41.672 --> 00:40:45.576
I mean, people were just
losing many, many pounds,


00:40:45.576 --> 00:40:47.311
And people--
It looked--


00:40:47.311 --> 00:40:48.612
People--It looked like,
you know,


00:40:49.046 --> 00:40:50.781
We were living
in a concentration camp.


00:40:51.215 --> 00:40:53.818
I mean, people were just
losing so much weight


00:40:53.818 --> 00:40:55.553
In their faces
and their bodies.


00:40:55.553 --> 00:40:58.589
You know, a third of their
body weight very, very quickly.


00:40:59.023 --> 00:41:00.758
Mysteriously.
They didn't know what was--


00:41:01.192 --> 00:41:03.360
You know, what part
of the disease was causing it.


00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:07.264
Um, so it was these
very physical manifestations


00:41:07.698 --> 00:41:11.168
That were horrifying to people,


00:41:11.168 --> 00:41:13.337
And were very scary to people,


00:41:13.771 --> 00:41:15.940
And if you--
Especially if you had aids,


00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:18.976
And then you saw somebody
who was much worse off than you,


00:41:18.976 --> 00:41:21.579
You almost had to turn away.


00:41:21.579 --> 00:41:25.049
It was just--
It was too scary.


00:41:25.049 --> 00:41:28.953
I was losing all the fat
in my face and my butt,


00:41:28.953 --> 00:41:30.254
And everywhere,


00:41:30.688 --> 00:41:33.290
And i would walk
by a store window


00:41:33.290 --> 00:41:35.459
And see myself in the window,
and just jump.


00:41:35.459 --> 00:41:37.194
It's like,
"who is that?"


00:41:37.194 --> 00:41:40.231
Um, and i remember
my mother saying,


00:41:40.664 --> 00:41:41.966
"Couldn't you
stand on your head,


00:41:41.966 --> 00:41:44.568
"And make some of the stuff
flow down to your face?


00:41:45.002 --> 00:41:45.870
You got nothing on your--"


00:41:46.303 --> 00:41:48.472
You know, "you're just--
You're skin and bones."


00:41:53.677 --> 00:41:57.147
- The aids epidemic allowed me
to move into the community


00:41:57.581 --> 00:41:59.750
In a very powerful way.


00:41:59.750 --> 00:42:02.353
And, in fact, in many ways,


00:42:02.353 --> 00:42:04.088
I began to thrive.


00:42:04.088 --> 00:42:08.425
Because it was, like,
being in the army.


00:42:08.859 --> 00:42:12.329
Like, i was really,
for the first time,


00:42:12.329 --> 00:42:14.932
Other than being
super involved in my family,


00:42:14.932 --> 00:42:17.535
I was involved
in something else.


00:42:17.535 --> 00:42:21.872
Like, i rolled up
my sleeves, and...


00:42:21.872 --> 00:42:25.776
i wanted to be
a part of this.


00:42:25.776 --> 00:42:29.680
The aids ward was a--


00:42:29.680 --> 00:42:34.885
It was a terrible and beautiful
place at the same time.


00:42:37.488 --> 00:42:41.392
My primary role was to be one of
the shanti counselors there,


00:42:41.392 --> 00:42:43.561
Which was someone
who was trained


00:42:43.561 --> 00:42:45.296
To be able to sit


00:42:45.729 --> 00:42:50.067
And be and witness
and have conversations


00:42:50.067 --> 00:42:55.706
And support people
through their process there.


00:42:56.140 --> 00:42:57.875
I worked with people there


00:42:57.875 --> 00:43:00.044
Who were, like,
18 years old.


00:43:00.044 --> 00:43:02.646
We had people there
who were in their '60s.


00:43:02.646 --> 00:43:09.153
But in general they were
sexually active gay men.


00:43:09.153 --> 00:43:10.888
People were coming
into the hospital


00:43:11.322 --> 00:43:14.358
With diseases like
toxoplasmosis,


00:43:14.358 --> 00:43:19.129
Which you can get from a potted
plant or a canary cage.


00:43:19.129 --> 00:43:23.467
I mean, people were
extremely susceptible


00:43:23.467 --> 00:43:25.636
To any number of things.


00:43:25.636 --> 00:43:32.142
So there had to be, like,
a controlled environment.


00:43:32.142 --> 00:43:37.348
There was this idea that
we were there to cure and heal,


00:43:37.348 --> 00:43:39.083
And--And not
to minimize any of that,


00:43:39.516 --> 00:43:42.553
But--But really, back then,


00:43:42.553 --> 00:43:43.420
What were people were doing is,


00:43:43.854 --> 00:43:45.155
They were dying of aids,


00:43:45.155 --> 00:43:48.192
And we were trying to help them
as best we could.


00:43:50.361 --> 00:43:51.662
You could go a couple days,


00:43:52.096 --> 00:43:56.867
And, um, no one would die.


00:43:56.867 --> 00:43:59.903
And then, in one day, like,
six people would die.


00:44:04.675 --> 00:44:09.013
We saw many
lover couples come in.


00:44:09.446 --> 00:44:10.748
One would die.


00:44:10.748 --> 00:44:12.916
The other, you know,
partner would be there,


00:44:12.916 --> 00:44:14.652
Go through the whole process,


00:44:14.652 --> 00:44:15.953
Some time would pass,


00:44:16.387 --> 00:44:20.724
And then the next lover
would come in.


00:44:21.158 --> 00:44:25.062
There was a mom
who came to 5-A,


00:44:25.062 --> 00:44:32.002
And one...two...three times,


00:44:32.002 --> 00:44:35.039
She lost her boys there.


00:44:39.810 --> 00:44:42.413
I would stand in the hallway,


00:44:42.846 --> 00:44:45.883
A gay man myself
in my mid 30s,


00:44:45.883 --> 00:44:49.353
Visiting and talking
to a mother and father


00:44:49.353 --> 00:44:51.522
Who had just stepped out
of a room,


00:44:51.522 --> 00:44:54.992
Who had just found out
that their son had pneumocystis


00:44:54.992 --> 00:44:58.028
And had three months to live,
or whatever,


00:44:58.462 --> 00:44:59.763
And the father
would stand there and go,


00:45:00.197 --> 00:45:03.667
"You know...


00:45:04.101 --> 00:45:07.571
"it's harder for me to find out
that my son is a fag


00:45:08.005 --> 00:45:13.210
Than to find out
that he's gonna be dying soon."


00:45:13.644 --> 00:45:16.680
And there i would be, like,
trying to comfort him.


00:45:22.753 --> 00:45:25.789
- When steve died,
my friends were there for me.


00:45:26.223 --> 00:45:27.524
I felt so supportive.


00:45:27.524 --> 00:45:30.994
My family was very--
Very much there for me.


00:45:31.428 --> 00:45:34.031
Also, i had other friends
who were sick,


00:45:34.031 --> 00:45:37.501
And so i--
It pulled me out of myself,


00:45:37.935 --> 00:45:41.839
'Cause i could go
help take care of them.


00:45:41.839 --> 00:45:43.574
I mean,
i think i mentioned peter,


00:45:43.574 --> 00:45:47.044
Who was one of my
dearest friends.


00:45:47.044 --> 00:45:48.779
He's one of the first
people i met


00:45:48.779 --> 00:45:50.514
When i moved to san francisco.


00:45:50.514 --> 00:45:52.249
He was tall and handsome,


00:45:52.249 --> 00:45:54.418
And grew up
in a trailer park,


00:45:54.418 --> 00:45:57.888
And he was--He used
to keep these diaries,


00:45:57.888 --> 00:46:00.491
And he always wanted them
published after he died


00:46:00.491 --> 00:46:04.394
As diaries of an
illiterate homosexual.


00:46:04.828 --> 00:46:06.130
Peter was such an original.


00:46:06.130 --> 00:46:09.600
He was just amazing.


00:46:09.600 --> 00:46:15.672
He died two weeks
after steve.


00:46:15.672 --> 00:46:17.407
He had moved
back here to die.


00:46:17.841 --> 00:46:18.709
He and his lover.


00:46:18.709 --> 00:46:20.444
I had introduced
he an his lover,


00:46:20.444 --> 00:46:22.179
George, and then
they moved to rhode island,


00:46:22.179 --> 00:46:23.480
Where george was from,


00:46:23.914 --> 00:46:25.215
And then when peter
started getting sick,


00:46:25.215 --> 00:46:26.517
They moved back
to san francisco,


00:46:26.950 --> 00:46:27.818
'Cause care was better,


00:46:28.252 --> 00:46:29.987
And their core group
of friends was here,


00:46:30.420 --> 00:46:33.891
And peter was getting
sicker and sicker,


00:46:33.891 --> 00:46:35.192
And they told him,
you know,


00:46:35.192 --> 00:46:37.361
You have, like,
four or five days to live,


00:46:37.361 --> 00:46:39.530
And he was just
in so much discomfort


00:46:39.963 --> 00:46:44.735
That he decided
to take his own life.


00:46:44.735 --> 00:46:47.771
So we, you know,
we got together all the drugs,


00:46:47.771 --> 00:46:52.109
And the cocktail
that was gonna kill him,


00:46:52.109 --> 00:46:56.013
And we had a party.
At his house.


00:46:56.013 --> 00:46:57.314
He was in bed.


00:46:57.314 --> 00:47:01.218
Sort of like a--
Like a queen holding court.


00:47:01.218 --> 00:47:07.724
And we each got to go up
and say our goodbyes.


00:47:07.724 --> 00:47:11.195
And i remember him saying,


00:47:11.195 --> 00:47:13.797
"You know,
when i was single,


00:47:14.231 --> 00:47:15.098
"You were married,


00:47:15.098 --> 00:47:17.267
"And when i was married,
you were single.


00:47:17.701 --> 00:47:20.737
"Did you think if we'd both
been single at the same time,


00:47:21.171 --> 00:47:22.906
We would have been partners?"


00:47:22.906 --> 00:47:26.376
And i said, "yeah.
I know we would have been."


00:47:26.376 --> 00:47:29.413
And then he gave me one
of the most passionate kisses


00:47:29.847 --> 00:47:32.883
I've ever had in my life.


00:47:35.485 --> 00:47:39.389
And then we all went away.


00:48:17.995 --> 00:48:21.031
- I was the charge nurse
in the medical clinic,


00:48:21.031 --> 00:48:24.935
And we were starting
the first a.Z.T. Trials,


00:48:24.935 --> 00:48:29.706
And dr. Jay had come on
to help that,


00:48:29.706 --> 00:48:31.441
And he looked at me one day,


00:48:31.875 --> 00:48:34.478
And he said,
"i think we can do this."


00:48:34.912 --> 00:48:37.514
We can do clinical research.


00:48:37.514 --> 00:48:39.683
And so we started


00:48:39.683 --> 00:48:44.454
The quest clinical research
center together.


00:48:44.888 --> 00:48:48.358
You know, both of us
had never done research.


00:48:48.358 --> 00:48:51.395
We just kind of did it,
you know?


00:48:51.828 --> 00:48:57.901
Back then there weren't
as many, um, regulations.


00:48:58.335 --> 00:49:01.371
The reason that you wanted
to do research back then


00:49:01.371 --> 00:49:02.673
Was because there was nothing,


00:49:03.106 --> 00:49:05.709
And all you were doing
was helping people die,


00:49:05.709 --> 00:49:09.179
And you just felt like you had
to work on these trials


00:49:09.179 --> 00:49:11.782
And, you know,
figure out what was working,


00:49:11.782 --> 00:49:13.517
Figure out
what the problems were


00:49:13.517 --> 00:49:15.252
And get these drugs approved


00:49:15.252 --> 00:49:17.854
So that everybody
could have 'em.


00:49:17.854 --> 00:49:20.891
By doing this
and working really hard


00:49:20.891 --> 00:49:23.060
And getting these drugs
on the market,


00:49:23.493 --> 00:49:27.831
You know,
maybe we could save lives.


00:49:28.265 --> 00:49:29.132
In the early days,


00:49:29.132 --> 00:49:30.867
I would go to people's houses.


00:49:30.867 --> 00:49:33.470
They were too sick to come in
to get their medicine,


00:49:33.470 --> 00:49:34.338
I'd go to their house.


00:49:34.771 --> 00:49:36.940
I'd draw their blood.


00:49:36.940 --> 00:49:39.543
They would come in
very educated,


00:49:39.977 --> 00:49:43.447
Wanting the newest treatment.


00:49:43.447 --> 00:49:45.615
Sometimes they would know
more than i did,


00:49:45.615 --> 00:49:48.652
'Cause they had, you know,
researched so much.


00:49:48.652 --> 00:49:50.821
And, um,
i would learn from them.


00:49:51.254 --> 00:49:55.158
There was really
a camaraderie there.


00:49:55.158 --> 00:49:58.195
You know, of course,
you know, we made mistakes.


00:49:58.195 --> 00:50:01.231
When, you know, when we first
started the azt trials,


00:50:01.665 --> 00:50:03.834
We were giving way too much,
you know.


00:50:03.834 --> 00:50:06.003
That's why people
got so sick on it,


00:50:06.436 --> 00:50:09.039
And it got a bad rap.


00:50:09.039 --> 00:50:10.774
If you ever
come to our office,


00:50:10.774 --> 00:50:13.810
We have this picture
of this guy


00:50:13.810 --> 00:50:17.280
Who is almost
like a skeleton,


00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:18.582
And he's holding a sign,


00:50:18.582 --> 00:50:21.184
"Man can't live on azt alone,"


00:50:21.618 --> 00:50:24.654
And every time
i see that picture,


00:50:24.654 --> 00:50:27.691
It brings me back
to those days of--


00:50:27.691 --> 00:50:30.293
We need more treatments.


00:50:30.727 --> 00:50:32.896
We need more than azt.


00:50:32.896 --> 00:50:38.969
And--And we need them
to happen quickly.


00:50:38.969 --> 00:50:42.005
- I remember one fellow
particularly said to me,


00:50:42.005 --> 00:50:44.174
"You know, i'm at the end
of my chemical rope,"


00:50:44.174 --> 00:50:48.078
Um, and i thought,
"boy, what a phrase from our--


00:50:48.512 --> 00:50:50.680
You know,
from this time."


00:50:51.114 --> 00:50:53.283
- These doctors were coming up


00:50:53.283 --> 00:50:55.886
With every kind of pill
that you should take.


00:50:55.886 --> 00:50:59.790
It seemed like every day they
were coming up with a new cure,


00:50:59.790 --> 00:51:02.392
But my friends
were guinea pigs,


00:51:02.826 --> 00:51:05.429
And those cures didn't work,


00:51:05.862 --> 00:51:08.899
And they were still dying,
and they were still dying,


00:51:08.899 --> 00:51:13.236
And not even just my friends,
my relatives.


00:51:13.236 --> 00:51:18.442
You know, my--My cousin,
he died of aids, you know?


00:51:18.875 --> 00:51:22.345
And it was, like, the whole
family kept it, you know,


00:51:22.345 --> 00:51:24.081
Zips the lip.


00:51:24.081 --> 00:51:28.418
Nobody wanted to say
that people were gay, you know,


00:51:28.418 --> 00:51:31.888
And we didn't speak about it.


00:51:31.888 --> 00:51:34.057
We just said romeo was sick.


00:51:34.057 --> 00:51:36.660
And, um,


00:51:36.660 --> 00:51:40.130
He just succumbed to...


00:51:40.130 --> 00:51:41.431
aids,


00:51:41.865 --> 00:51:44.901
And he died.
Very quietly.


00:52:06.156 --> 00:52:09.626
- I think my biggest fears
around getting sick


00:52:09.626 --> 00:52:13.530
Was...blindness.


00:52:13.530 --> 00:52:17.000
There was a lot,
in the early days of aids,


00:52:17.000 --> 00:52:20.036
Of c.M.V.,
cytomegalovirus,


00:52:20.036 --> 00:52:21.771
Which attack the eyes,


00:52:21.771 --> 00:52:23.507
And people were
losing their eyesight


00:52:23.940 --> 00:52:25.675
In a short period of time.


00:52:26.109 --> 00:52:29.146
And...


00:52:29.579 --> 00:52:30.881
you know,
i could deal with pain,


00:52:30.881 --> 00:52:31.748
Or they could, you know,


00:52:32.182 --> 00:52:33.483
They could manage pain
and all that,


00:52:33.483 --> 00:52:35.652
But the idea
of losing my eyesight


00:52:35.652 --> 00:52:40.423
Was really--I think it really,
really scared me.


00:52:40.857 --> 00:52:43.460
- We worked on this trial


00:52:43.894 --> 00:52:46.062
For cmv retinitis.


00:52:46.062 --> 00:52:49.966
It affected,
infected people's eyes.


00:52:50.400 --> 00:52:52.569
We wanted to do research,


00:52:52.569 --> 00:52:57.774
So we would ask them if we could
take their eyes when they died.


00:52:58.208 --> 00:53:00.377
And...


00:53:00.377 --> 00:53:03.847
you know, that was a hard
conversation to have,


00:53:03.847 --> 00:53:05.582
But people were into it.


00:53:06.016 --> 00:53:08.618
They were going,
"this awful thing is happening,


00:53:09.052 --> 00:53:13.390
"And, you know, if i can give
my eyes to advance this,


00:53:13.823 --> 00:53:15.559
I'm willing to do that."


00:53:15.992 --> 00:53:18.161
Any time anybody is ill,


00:53:18.161 --> 00:53:22.065
You're meeting them at a very
vulnerable place in their life,


00:53:22.065 --> 00:53:27.704
And these relationships can grow
very intensely very quickly,


00:53:27.704 --> 00:53:35.946
So it was my job to go
into the autopsy room,


00:53:35.946 --> 00:53:40.717
Um, when the pathologist
would come and remove the eyes,


00:53:40.717 --> 00:53:45.055
And, uh, i would have
to put them in this little,


00:53:45.055 --> 00:53:47.657
Like, urine container,


00:53:47.657 --> 00:53:50.694
And then put them
in a paper bag,


00:53:50.694 --> 00:53:54.164
And take them to the lab.


00:53:54.164 --> 00:53:57.634
And that was really,
really hard.


00:53:58.068 --> 00:54:00.670
I mean, these were people
i really knew,


00:54:00.670 --> 00:54:05.008
And, uh, loved, liked,
whatever you want to say,


00:54:05.008 --> 00:54:09.779
And it was really hard
to, um, watch this.


00:54:09.779 --> 00:54:14.117
And something that
i'll never forget, actually.


00:54:14.117 --> 00:54:18.455
But one of my patients'
sisters really helped me.


00:54:18.455 --> 00:54:24.961
Because she said to me
something like,


00:54:24.961 --> 00:54:26.696
"It makes me feel better


00:54:27.130 --> 00:54:28.865
"To know that you're
gonna be with him


00:54:28.865 --> 00:54:30.600
"When this is happening,


00:54:30.600 --> 00:54:35.372
"That i was there
to watch over these people,


00:54:35.372 --> 00:54:38.408
"And make sure
they were treated with respect,


00:54:38.842 --> 00:54:41.878
And that their body
was handled with love,"


00:54:41.878 --> 00:54:47.951
And i just was so grateful
for her to give me that job.


00:55:29.159 --> 00:55:31.761
- How deeply are americans
worried about aids?


00:55:31.761 --> 00:55:33.063
Alos angeles timespoll


00:55:33.496 --> 00:55:34.798
Found that 50% of americans


00:55:34.798 --> 00:55:37.400
Favor quarantine
for aids victims.


00:55:37.400 --> 00:55:41.304
48% said they should be
issued special identification.


00:55:41.304 --> 00:55:45.642
15% said aids victims
should be tattooed.


00:55:45.642 --> 00:55:49.112
We were preoccupied
for those first four years


00:55:49.112 --> 00:55:50.847
With extraordinary
civil rights attacks.


00:55:51.281 --> 00:55:53.883
In 1986 in california,


00:55:53.883 --> 00:55:56.920
There was an initiative on
the ballot by lyndon larouche,


00:55:56.920 --> 00:56:00.390
And it was an initiative
to enforce the quarantine laws


00:56:00.390 --> 00:56:03.426
Relative to hiv in california.


00:56:03.860 --> 00:56:07.764
And it was--It was written
in such a way to sound medical,


00:56:07.764 --> 00:56:11.234
But the intent as interpreted
by the queer community


00:56:11.668 --> 00:56:13.403
And everyone else was


00:56:13.403 --> 00:56:16.873
This is to stigmatize people
with hiv aids,


00:56:16.873 --> 00:56:19.909
And could go so far
as to have them


00:56:19.909 --> 00:56:22.078
Be quarantined
under doctor's orders.


00:56:22.078 --> 00:56:24.681
And when that ballot initiative
first was put forward,


00:56:25.115 --> 00:56:27.717
Um, it was
overwhelmingly favored.


00:56:28.151 --> 00:56:30.320
It was overwhelmingly favored.


00:56:30.754 --> 00:56:34.224
And a statewide
campaign formed,


00:56:34.224 --> 00:56:36.393
And we organized
throughout california


00:56:36.393 --> 00:56:38.995
To defeat the initiative,
and defeated it.


00:56:38.995 --> 00:56:40.730
And it came again
two years later.


00:56:40.730 --> 00:56:42.899
It was put forward
a second time.


00:56:43.333 --> 00:56:45.502
Um, in--In '88.


00:56:45.502 --> 00:56:48.538
And simultaneously,
there were laws that were--


00:56:48.538 --> 00:56:51.141
That was people could be fired
for being hiv positive.


00:56:51.141 --> 00:56:53.309
People could be
mandatory tested.


00:56:53.309 --> 00:56:55.478
Other words, you could be tested
without your consent,


00:56:55.912 --> 00:56:58.515
Um, and then those results
made available to people.


00:56:59.816 --> 00:57:01.117
Fact is,
the reagan administration


00:57:01.117 --> 00:57:02.419
Has been criminal
in its response,


00:57:02.852 --> 00:57:04.154
And they've done so
because they thought


00:57:04.154 --> 00:57:05.455
It was a disease
of the gay community.


00:57:05.889 --> 00:57:08.491
And what needs to be done
is a federal program


00:57:08.925 --> 00:57:10.226
That's equivalent to our effort
to get to the moon,


00:57:10.660 --> 00:57:12.829
That is equivalent of our effort
to develop the atomic bomb.


00:57:12.829 --> 00:57:15.432
If we implement that,
we can stop aids,


00:57:15.432 --> 00:57:17.600
But the way to go is not to
start violating civil rights.


00:57:17.600 --> 00:57:18.902
I mean, the way to go


00:57:18.902 --> 00:57:21.070
Is not to start turning
american against american


00:57:21.070 --> 00:57:21.938
In times of crisis.


00:57:22.372 --> 00:57:24.107
- And i believe
that when you live immorally,


00:57:24.107 --> 00:57:25.842
Whether you're
a heterosexual or a homosexual,


00:57:26.276 --> 00:57:27.143
And you violate
the laws of god,


00:57:27.577 --> 00:57:28.878
And homosexuality does,


00:57:28.878 --> 00:57:35.819
You become wide open to every
kind of sin and sickness.


00:57:35.819 --> 00:57:39.289
- I think the country
as a whole understood


00:57:39.289 --> 00:57:42.759
That the queer community
was taking care of each other,


00:57:42.759 --> 00:57:44.928
That our principal response


00:57:45.361 --> 00:57:48.398
Was food banks
and care programs,


00:57:48.832 --> 00:57:51.868
And that it was a response
that america should be proud of,


00:57:51.868 --> 00:57:54.904
And that maybe
the pat buchanans


00:57:54.904 --> 00:57:57.073
And the bigots
who were attacking us


00:57:57.507 --> 00:58:00.543
And who basically just
wanted us to die were wrong.


00:58:00.543 --> 00:58:01.845
And at a certain point,


00:58:01.845 --> 00:58:04.013
Those--Those attacks
just stopped.


00:58:04.447 --> 00:58:07.050
They just couldn't
get traction


00:58:07.050 --> 00:58:11.821
To continue to stigmatize
people with aids.


00:58:11.821 --> 00:58:15.291
- Aids organizations were just
popping up everywhere.


00:58:15.291 --> 00:58:16.159
I mean, that was--


00:58:16.159 --> 00:58:17.894
It was called
the san francisco model.


00:58:17.894 --> 00:58:20.930
I think one of the reasons
the san francisco model worked


00:58:21.364 --> 00:58:23.533
Was 'cause of the size
of san francisco,


00:58:23.533 --> 00:58:26.135
And because of
castro street itself,


00:58:26.569 --> 00:58:31.341
That there was a center.


00:58:31.341 --> 00:58:34.811
San francisco people
came here not for career.


00:58:34.811 --> 00:58:38.715
They came here because
they wanted to live here.


00:58:38.715 --> 00:58:42.185
And when aids came along,


00:58:42.185 --> 00:58:44.787
The community was sort of
inherent in that.


00:58:44.787 --> 00:58:47.390
It--All it needed
was the aids epidemic


00:58:47.824 --> 00:58:50.426
To really make it coalesce.


00:58:50.426 --> 00:58:53.029
Whether it was taking care
of peoples' pets


00:58:53.463 --> 00:58:54.764
When they were
in the hospital,


00:58:54.764 --> 00:58:57.367
Or bringing them food,
like open hand,


00:58:57.367 --> 00:58:59.969
Everybody wanted
to do something.


00:58:59.969 --> 00:59:03.006
It was a way
the community came together


00:59:03.006 --> 00:59:05.608
In an amazing way that...


00:59:05.608 --> 00:59:09.078
you know,
politics had never done that.


00:59:09.078 --> 00:59:11.681
And it brought together
the women's community


00:59:11.681 --> 00:59:14.284
And the gay women's community
and the gay male community


00:59:14.717 --> 00:59:19.923
In ways that had certainly
never happened before.


00:59:20.356 --> 00:59:22.091
- Again and again,
in every situation,


00:59:22.525 --> 00:59:25.128
Every circumstance,
there's lesbians there


00:59:25.128 --> 00:59:27.297
Leading the fight.


00:59:27.297 --> 00:59:30.333
All the women had friends
who were gay guys


00:59:30.333 --> 00:59:32.502
Who were sick.


00:59:32.936 --> 00:59:35.538
I was walking up castro street
one day to my apartment,


00:59:35.538 --> 00:59:38.141
And in the early days
of these horrible tests,


00:59:38.141 --> 00:59:41.611
People would become anemic,
severely anemic.


00:59:41.611 --> 00:59:42.912
There was also
a blood shortage,


00:59:42.912 --> 00:59:45.515
Because of hiv and blood.


00:59:45.515 --> 00:59:48.117
Lesbians weren't at risk
for hiv,


00:59:48.117 --> 00:59:50.720
And--And could donate blood,
and did.


00:59:50.720 --> 00:59:52.455
And so i'm walking up
castro street,


00:59:52.455 --> 00:59:53.756
And i see a poster,


00:59:53.756 --> 00:59:55.491
And i believe it was from
the lesbian caucus


00:59:55.491 --> 00:59:56.793
Of the harvey milk
gay democratic club,


00:59:57.226 --> 00:59:59.395
And it said
"our boys need blood.


00:59:59.829 --> 01:00:01.564
"Lesbian caucus blood drive


01:00:01.998 --> 01:00:04.167
For people with aids,
san francisco."


01:00:04.167 --> 01:00:06.336
And i remember thinking,


01:00:06.769 --> 01:00:11.107
"This is just
a wonderful thing."


01:00:11.107 --> 01:00:13.710
- People came
to san francisco to go,


01:00:14.143 --> 01:00:21.084
"What is happening here that
the response is so heartfelt?"


01:00:21.517 --> 01:00:24.988
I think what made 5-A
such a spectacular place


01:00:24.988 --> 01:00:26.289
And such a powerful response


01:00:26.723 --> 01:00:28.458
Were the people
who worked there.


01:00:28.891 --> 01:00:30.627
It's also true
of shanti project.


01:00:30.627 --> 01:00:33.229
I mean--
I mean, literally,


01:00:33.229 --> 01:00:34.530
It was thousands of people


01:00:34.530 --> 01:00:38.001
Who volunteered
thousands of hours.


01:00:38.001 --> 01:00:41.471
[Big band music,
tap shoes clicking]


01:00:45.375 --> 01:00:48.411
- Every other sunday,
there is a party on ward 5-B.


01:00:48.411 --> 01:00:51.447
The hostess is a travel agent
named rita berger,


01:00:51.447 --> 01:00:55.351
But the nurses and the patients
know her as rita rocket.


01:00:55.351 --> 01:01:00.990
- She came on an easter
to offer to do an easter brunch.


01:01:01.424 --> 01:01:05.762
It went so well, turned into,
like, she would come on sundays.


01:01:05.762 --> 01:01:12.268
And she would come
with this whole group of men,


01:01:12.268 --> 01:01:14.003
Who spent
a good part of the week,


01:01:14.437 --> 01:01:19.642
Like, baking all the food
that was gonna be eaten.


01:01:20.076 --> 01:01:23.980
[Cheers and applause]


01:01:23.980 --> 01:01:26.149
- I got together
with some friends,


01:01:26.149 --> 01:01:29.619
And we started an organization
called visual aid.


01:01:29.619 --> 01:01:30.486
I thought, okay, you know,


01:01:30.920 --> 01:01:33.089
Just start in
the community that i know,


01:01:33.089 --> 01:01:34.390
Which is artists,


01:01:34.390 --> 01:01:36.559
And i was seeing
artist friends


01:01:36.559 --> 01:01:40.897
Who were having to make
the choice between...


01:01:40.897 --> 01:01:44.801
medical care
or art supplies.


01:01:44.801 --> 01:01:46.536
When art was--


01:01:46.969 --> 01:01:47.837
You know,
it was also therapy.


01:01:48.271 --> 01:01:49.572
It keeps you going.


01:01:49.572 --> 01:01:53.042
So we started this
organization called visual aid,


01:01:53.476 --> 01:01:58.681
Which would give artists
access to art supplies.


01:01:58.681 --> 01:01:59.982
We had great t-Shirts,


01:01:59.982 --> 01:02:00.850
And we would sell them


01:02:01.284 --> 01:02:03.886
At every street fair
gay pride parade,


01:02:04.320 --> 01:02:05.621
And we actually
made a lot of money,


01:02:06.055 --> 01:02:08.224
And i remember
in one meeting saying,


01:02:08.224 --> 01:02:09.525
"You know, christmastime


01:02:09.525 --> 01:02:11.694
"Is a time when people
are buying lots of gifts,


01:02:11.694 --> 01:02:12.995
"And there's no street fairs.


01:02:12.995 --> 01:02:14.297
"We should do
something about that.


01:02:14.731 --> 01:02:16.466
We should have a place that
we could sell these things."


01:02:16.899 --> 01:02:21.237
And so i had this idea
to start a store.


01:02:21.237 --> 01:02:23.406
I had pulled together
a board of directors,


01:02:23.406 --> 01:02:28.177
And they wanted
to name it aids mart.


01:02:28.177 --> 01:02:30.346
And i said, "no."


01:02:30.780 --> 01:02:32.949
I said,
"i'm gonna pull rank here.


01:02:32.949 --> 01:02:36.853
I'm the president, and it's not
gonna be called aids mart."


01:02:36.853 --> 01:02:40.757
They said, "aids mart.
Aid smart. See?"


01:02:40.757 --> 01:02:42.058
And i said, "no.


01:02:42.058 --> 01:02:45.094
Nobody's gonna shop
at a store called aids mart."


01:02:45.094 --> 01:02:47.263
But "under one roof"
just sounded right.


01:02:47.263 --> 01:02:49.866
I remember
working the cash register,


01:02:50.299 --> 01:02:52.468
And, you know,
when you're working at a store,


01:02:52.468 --> 01:02:55.071
You usually say thank you
to the customer.


01:02:55.071 --> 01:02:58.107
I swear, every customer
would just say "thank you.


01:02:58.541 --> 01:03:00.710
Thank you for doing this."


01:03:00.710 --> 01:03:02.879
'Cause, you know,
people who weren't


01:03:03.312 --> 01:03:05.481
Doing anything in the
community felt so powerless,


01:03:05.915 --> 01:03:08.951
And here was one even
little way, by shopping,


01:03:08.951 --> 01:03:13.723
By buying a mug or a t-Shirt
for their aunt tillie.


01:03:14.157 --> 01:03:15.024
What ended up happening is,


01:03:15.458 --> 01:03:18.060
Most of our volunteers
were people with aids


01:03:18.060 --> 01:03:19.796
Who were on disability.


01:03:19.796 --> 01:03:21.097
People were sick.


01:03:21.097 --> 01:03:23.266
But they could get out of bed
one day a week


01:03:23.266 --> 01:03:25.001
And work the cash register.


01:03:25.001 --> 01:03:28.471
And it became,
for a lot of our volunteers,


01:03:28.471 --> 01:03:29.338
Their social life,


01:03:29.772 --> 01:03:34.544
Their only time
out of their houses.


01:03:34.544 --> 01:03:38.447
- I felt as though
we were more compassionate.


01:03:38.447 --> 01:03:39.749
We were going through things


01:03:39.749 --> 01:03:41.918
That other people
didn't go through,


01:03:41.918 --> 01:03:43.653
Other people
didn't understand.


01:03:43.653 --> 01:03:45.388
It just went over
everybody's head.


01:03:45.388 --> 01:03:49.725
And i just remember


01:03:49.725 --> 01:03:53.196
How close that brought
everybody together.


01:03:53.196 --> 01:03:56.666
You know, it was just, like,
we didn't care who you were,


01:03:56.666 --> 01:03:59.702
But we all had
the same burden.


01:03:59.702 --> 01:04:01.871
And that was just, like,


01:04:02.305 --> 01:04:04.473
It was just, like, the glue.


01:04:04.473 --> 01:04:08.811
- Gay people were never seen
as care givers.


01:04:08.811 --> 01:04:11.848
They were seen as, you know,
good time people, you know,


01:04:11.848 --> 01:04:13.583
Having fun, being wild.


01:04:13.583 --> 01:04:18.354
And, all of a sudden, we were
the ultimate care givers.


01:04:18.354 --> 01:04:22.258
It changed people's view
of the gay community


01:04:22.692 --> 01:04:25.728
In a huge way.


01:04:26.162 --> 01:04:27.897
I remember
my father saying,


01:04:27.897 --> 01:04:32.235
'Cause i was spending so much
time taking care of my friends.


01:04:32.235 --> 01:04:35.271
And he was saying,
"these aren't family."


01:04:35.705 --> 01:04:40.476
And i said,
"yes, they are.


01:04:40.476 --> 01:04:42.645
This is my family."


01:04:43.079 --> 01:04:44.380
And he got it.


01:04:44.380 --> 01:04:47.416
He ended up taking care
of my friends too.


01:04:50.887 --> 01:04:52.622
[Dramatic music]


01:04:53.055 --> 01:05:00.863
♪ ♪


01:05:40.770 --> 01:05:43.806
- When i was
in the thick of it,


01:05:43.806 --> 01:05:49.011
I became, and i suspect
many people like me did,


01:05:49.445 --> 01:05:51.614
Whether you were infected
or uninfected,


01:05:51.614 --> 01:05:54.650
It was hard
to imagine the future.


01:05:56.819 --> 01:06:02.458
I didn't look much further
than the next week or two,


01:06:02.458 --> 01:06:05.494
Because the whole thing
was so...


01:06:05.928 --> 01:06:11.133
impossible to grasp that
all this was really happening.


01:06:11.133 --> 01:06:19.375
I went into a long period
of being isolated, very sad.


01:06:19.375 --> 01:06:21.110
You know,
all the years at 5-A,


01:06:21.544 --> 01:06:23.279
And all of the death
and dying years


01:06:23.279 --> 01:06:28.050
Had really
taken its toll on me.


01:06:28.050 --> 01:06:30.653
I had been there,
you know, for three years,


01:06:30.653 --> 01:06:32.822
And it--
And it did cross my mind.


01:06:32.822 --> 01:06:37.593
Like, "wow, how do you...
how do you stop?


01:06:37.593 --> 01:06:42.365
How do you stop working
in a place like this?"


01:06:42.365 --> 01:06:46.268
We have a local newspaper
here in san francisco


01:06:46.268 --> 01:06:48.004
Called thebay area reporter,


01:06:48.437 --> 01:06:51.474
And there was one issue.


01:06:51.474 --> 01:06:55.811
They decided to run
just all the photos


01:06:55.811 --> 01:07:00.149
Of the people that had died
in the last year.


01:07:05.788 --> 01:07:10.126
It was just page
after page after page


01:07:10.559 --> 01:07:12.728
After page.


01:07:13.162 --> 01:07:15.765
Of all of these
primarily gay men


01:07:15.765 --> 01:07:18.801
Who had died on the unit.


01:07:23.139 --> 01:07:26.175
I just felt something,
like, right here.


01:07:26.609 --> 01:07:30.513
It was a physical,
like, click.


01:07:30.946 --> 01:07:32.681
Because i saw all these faces,


01:07:33.115 --> 01:07:37.019
And i was stunned.


01:07:37.019 --> 01:07:43.959
By how many of them i knew
from working on the unit.


01:07:43.959 --> 01:07:48.297
And i, you know, i realized


01:07:48.297 --> 01:07:50.032
I couldn't.


01:07:50.032 --> 01:07:53.936
I just couldn't--
Couldn't do it anymore.


01:08:00.009 --> 01:08:02.178
- There's times
when you just think,


01:08:02.178 --> 01:08:04.780
"I can't take it anymore.


01:08:04.780 --> 01:08:06.082
"I don't want to watch this.


01:08:06.082 --> 01:08:07.383
"I don't want to see it.


01:08:07.383 --> 01:08:09.118
"There's just too many images


01:08:09.118 --> 01:08:12.154
That i don't want in my head."


01:08:12.154 --> 01:08:18.227
And, you know, your feeling
of wanting to run away.


01:08:18.661 --> 01:08:22.998
It was my generation
that was being infected,


01:08:22.998 --> 01:08:26.469
And so that, of course,
made it even heavier,


01:08:26.469 --> 01:08:30.806
Because, you know,
we were way too young to die.


01:08:30.806 --> 01:08:35.144
And i felt like i was too young
to go through all this.


01:08:35.578 --> 01:08:39.482
Why--You know,
all this loss.


01:08:39.482 --> 01:08:42.952
When you're doing this work,


01:08:42.952 --> 01:08:46.422
You have to figure out
how to take care of yourself


01:08:46.422 --> 01:08:50.326
And not feel it all the time.


01:08:50.326 --> 01:08:52.928
But sometimes
when somebody would die,


01:08:53.362 --> 01:08:55.531
And i'd find myself crying,


01:08:55.531 --> 01:08:58.567
I would feel like
i was crying for everyone.


01:08:58.567 --> 01:09:00.302
It wasn't just that person.


01:09:00.736 --> 01:09:03.339
It just felt overwhelming,


01:09:03.339 --> 01:09:05.508
And i just--


01:09:05.941 --> 01:09:08.110
'Cause sometimes
you just really had to cry.


01:09:08.544 --> 01:09:10.713
You had to let it out.


01:09:13.749 --> 01:09:15.918
- I think there were
probably some times


01:09:15.918 --> 01:09:17.219
During the epidemic for me


01:09:17.219 --> 01:09:18.954
Where i would hear
somebody was sick,


01:09:19.388 --> 01:09:23.726
And it was just--
I wouldn't call them,


01:09:24.160 --> 01:09:25.461
Or i'd just--
I couldn't see them.


01:09:25.461 --> 01:09:26.762
It was just too much.


01:09:26.762 --> 01:09:30.666
It was just, like, i...


01:09:30.666 --> 01:09:32.835
somehow knew my limits,


01:09:33.269 --> 01:09:36.305
And i couldn't take
one more sick friend...


01:09:36.305 --> 01:09:37.606
on.


01:09:37.606 --> 01:09:41.510
And it felt bad.


01:09:41.510 --> 01:09:43.245
But it was--It's so easy


01:09:43.245 --> 01:09:45.414
To just become part
of a caregiver's group


01:09:45.414 --> 01:09:47.149
And, you know,
that's your life


01:09:47.149 --> 01:09:49.752
For the next
many, many months,


01:09:49.752 --> 01:09:53.656
And sometimes
i just couldn't do it.


01:09:54.089 --> 01:09:56.692
Especially during
the late '80s and early '90s.


01:09:56.692 --> 01:10:00.162
I was sick,
and it was just enough


01:10:00.596 --> 01:10:03.199
To get, you know,
get me out of bed.


01:10:03.199 --> 01:10:05.801
A lot of times, it was
the side effects of the drugs.


01:10:05.801 --> 01:10:08.404
It wasn't just
the disease itself.


01:10:08.404 --> 01:10:09.705
You're just so caught up
with dealing--


01:10:10.139 --> 01:10:15.344
Whether it's nausea or wasting
or dizziness or fatigue,


01:10:15.344 --> 01:10:17.079
That you don't have time


01:10:17.079 --> 01:10:18.380
To worry about
what else could happen.


01:10:18.380 --> 01:10:21.850
It's just you're dealing with
what is happening.


01:10:21.850 --> 01:10:24.887
Tim was my partner
during this time,


01:10:25.321 --> 01:10:27.056
But he was also hiv positive,


01:10:27.489 --> 01:10:30.960
And i just didn't think
i could do it again.


01:10:30.960 --> 01:10:33.128
I could not
lose another partner,


01:10:33.562 --> 01:10:34.863
And i told him that.


01:10:34.863 --> 01:10:35.731
But we liked each other.


01:10:36.165 --> 01:10:37.900
We had really good
times together.


01:10:37.900 --> 01:10:39.201
And we kept
seeing each other,


01:10:39.635 --> 01:10:41.370
And after about six months,


01:10:41.804 --> 01:10:46.575
He said, you know,
"are we together, or aren't we?


01:10:46.575 --> 01:10:47.876
Are you here,
or aren't you?"


01:10:48.310 --> 01:10:49.612
And i just, you know, said,


01:10:49.612 --> 01:10:52.648
"You know,
i really love this person,


01:10:52.648 --> 01:10:56.552
And what happens,
you know, happens."


01:10:56.552 --> 01:10:59.154
We would sort of
take turns being sick.


01:10:59.154 --> 01:11:00.456
You know,
i would get really sick,


01:11:00.456 --> 01:11:01.757
And then
he would take care of me,


01:11:02.191 --> 01:11:04.360
Then he would get really sick,
and i would take care of him,


01:11:04.360 --> 01:11:08.264
And thank god we were never
both sick at the same time.


01:11:08.697 --> 01:11:09.999
He was not feeling well,


01:11:09.999 --> 01:11:11.300
And i called the doctor,


01:11:11.734 --> 01:11:13.035
And i said,
"i'm going to the hospital."


01:11:13.035 --> 01:11:15.204
And i bundled him
into the car


01:11:15.638 --> 01:11:19.108
And drive him down
dolores street.


01:11:21.277 --> 01:11:23.879
He...i guess an aneurysm.


01:11:23.879 --> 01:11:26.482
He just--His mouth
just locked shut,


01:11:26.482 --> 01:11:30.386
And there i am driving, like,
80 miles an hour


01:11:30.819 --> 01:11:32.554
Down dolores street,


01:11:32.554 --> 01:11:34.723
And try to pry his mouth open,


01:11:35.157 --> 01:11:37.760
Just saying,
"breathe, breathe."


01:11:37.760 --> 01:11:41.230
And we were supposed
to go to cpmc,


01:11:41.230 --> 01:11:43.399
But i knew that was
way too far away,


01:11:43.399 --> 01:11:46.435
And davies was closer,
and i just--I mean,


01:11:46.435 --> 01:11:48.604
Thank god i didn't
kill anybody on the street.


01:11:48.604 --> 01:11:49.905
I was really going down--


01:11:49.905 --> 01:11:52.074
Running every light
on dolores street,


01:11:52.074 --> 01:11:57.713
Just honking my horn,
just driving.


01:11:57.713 --> 01:12:01.617
And by the time
i got to davies, he was dead.


01:12:01.617 --> 01:12:03.352
It was so quick.


01:12:03.352 --> 01:12:06.388
I was in
a total state of shock.


01:12:13.329 --> 01:12:17.666
I thought i was gonna
lose my mind.


01:12:17.666 --> 01:12:21.570
Just felt like
it would be real easy


01:12:21.570 --> 01:12:23.739
To just not be here anymore.


01:12:23.739 --> 01:12:25.474
Most of my friends were dead.


01:12:25.908 --> 01:12:30.245
And there just didn't seem to be
any reason to stick around.


01:12:30.245 --> 01:12:33.282
But i didn't, and i'm really
glad i didn't kill myself.


01:12:33.716 --> 01:12:36.318
But it was--It's the only time
i've ever been suicidal.


01:12:36.752 --> 01:12:38.487
It just--And it was odd.


01:12:38.921 --> 01:12:40.656
It wasn't...


01:12:41.090 --> 01:12:42.391
it wasn't a crazy suicidal.


01:12:42.391 --> 01:12:44.993
It just felt very, like,


01:12:44.993 --> 01:12:47.596
"I don't--" You know,
"i don't need to be here.


01:12:47.596 --> 01:12:49.765
There's no reason
for me to be here."


01:12:49.765 --> 01:12:52.368
It seemed very logical.


01:12:52.368 --> 01:12:56.271
Um, i still could understand it,
looking back.


01:13:30.105 --> 01:13:31.407
- There was some hope
on treatment,


01:13:31.407 --> 01:13:32.708
Some hope on research.


01:13:32.708 --> 01:13:34.877
Some of the money
had begun to flow,


01:13:34.877 --> 01:13:37.913
And it had paid off
with some early drugs.


01:13:37.913 --> 01:13:40.082
Experimental drugs
were more accessible.


01:13:40.082 --> 01:13:43.118
Gay activists were meeting
with pharmaceutical companies


01:13:43.118 --> 01:13:45.287
To actually
talk about medicines.


01:13:45.287 --> 01:13:47.456
So, yeah, there was--
And then act up comes.


01:13:47.456 --> 01:13:49.625
It was, like, this wave
of sort of brilliant,


01:13:50.058 --> 01:13:53.529
Young,
artistic new yorkers.


01:13:53.529 --> 01:13:54.830
Uh, thing about act up,


01:13:55.264 --> 01:13:56.999
Is it's true
they were political,


01:13:56.999 --> 01:13:58.300
But they were
political artists.


01:13:58.300 --> 01:13:59.601
From their very
opening statement,


01:14:00.035 --> 01:14:00.903
"Silence equals death,"


01:14:00.903 --> 01:14:02.204
It's art, it's culture,


01:14:02.204 --> 01:14:06.542
And it was, you know,
it transformed the dialogue.


01:14:06.542 --> 01:14:08.277
- 60,000 deaths remain!


01:14:08.710 --> 01:14:11.313
Where was george?


01:14:11.313 --> 01:14:13.048
- Fight back!
Fight aids!


01:14:13.048 --> 01:14:15.217
- Healthcare is awry!


01:14:15.217 --> 01:14:19.121
Healthcare is awry!


01:14:19.121 --> 01:14:22.591
- That was the first time
i crossed a picket line.


01:14:22.591 --> 01:14:26.061
I wanted to go in
to the aids conference,


01:14:26.061 --> 01:14:30.399
Because there was information
i wanted to get inside.


01:14:30.399 --> 01:14:32.568
And what they were screaming
and hollering about,


01:14:32.568 --> 01:14:33.869
I agreed with.


01:14:33.869 --> 01:14:36.038
So--But then i realized


01:14:36.038 --> 01:14:39.942
That everybody is doing
what they need to do.


01:14:40.375 --> 01:14:41.677
They need to be out there


01:14:42.110 --> 01:14:43.846
Screaming and hollering
and pushing,


01:14:44.279 --> 01:14:46.882
Because things don't happen
unless you push.


01:14:46.882 --> 01:14:49.485
And i needed to go in
to get that information


01:14:49.485 --> 01:14:51.220
So i could take care of them.


01:14:51.220 --> 01:14:54.690
And--So it made me--
Once i figured that out,


01:14:55.123 --> 01:14:57.726
It was a little easier
to cross that picket line.


01:14:57.726 --> 01:15:00.762
- Act up! Fight back!
Fight aids!


01:15:00.762 --> 01:15:05.100
- I mean, that was when drugs
weren't on the fast track,


01:15:05.100 --> 01:15:08.136
Where it took ten years
to get a drug approved,


01:15:08.136 --> 01:15:09.872
And the activists
really worked


01:15:09.872 --> 01:15:12.908
For that to change.


01:15:17.679 --> 01:15:21.149
- Neil jaeger.


01:15:21.149 --> 01:15:24.620
James martin case.


01:15:25.053 --> 01:15:27.656
- One of the ways
i came back into the world


01:15:27.656 --> 01:15:29.825
Was through the names project,


01:15:30.259 --> 01:15:32.427
Which is the aids
memorial quilt,


01:15:32.427 --> 01:15:35.030
Which cleve jones started.


01:15:35.030 --> 01:15:38.934
- And my friend,
marvin feldman.


01:15:38.934 --> 01:15:42.404
- He came up with the idea
that people would make panels,


01:15:42.404 --> 01:15:47.175
Memorializing their friends
and children and lovers.


01:15:47.175 --> 01:15:50.646
It was a creative, positive way
to focus their grief,


01:15:51.079 --> 01:15:52.381
Then sew it all together,


01:15:52.381 --> 01:15:58.453
And make a powerful,
political statement.


01:15:58.453 --> 01:16:01.924
- When they went to washington
and unfolded those blankets,


01:16:01.924 --> 01:16:05.394
It was like,
you know, to me,


01:16:05.394 --> 01:16:08.864
Lotus flower after lotus flower
after lotus flower,


01:16:08.864 --> 01:16:11.466
And each petal was a person,
you know?


01:16:11.900 --> 01:16:13.635
And it was so powerful.


01:16:14.069 --> 01:16:14.937
It was so powerful.


01:16:15.370 --> 01:16:17.973
You didn't even
have to say anything.


01:16:17.973 --> 01:16:21.009
The tears would just come.


01:16:30.118 --> 01:16:30.986
- How are you?


01:16:30.986 --> 01:16:33.589
- I'm good.
Nervous, but good.


01:16:33.589 --> 01:16:35.757
- Sure.
Results are negative.


01:16:36.191 --> 01:16:37.926
- Okay. Good.


01:16:37.926 --> 01:16:38.794
- Good.
- Good.


01:16:39.227 --> 01:16:42.264
- I still wanted
to be involved.


01:16:42.698 --> 01:16:43.999
After my work
in the hospital,


01:16:44.433 --> 01:16:47.903
It was fairly easy
for me to translate,


01:16:47.903 --> 01:16:49.638
Take those skills,


01:16:49.638 --> 01:16:53.542
And move into working
in testing clinics,


01:16:53.976 --> 01:16:56.578
And working with people
who are at risk for hiv,


01:16:57.012 --> 01:16:59.615
As well as occasionally
having to tell people


01:16:59.615 --> 01:17:01.783
That they were infected.


01:17:01.783 --> 01:17:03.518
- When the test occurred,


01:17:03.952 --> 01:17:04.820
One of the main
things we could do


01:17:05.253 --> 01:17:06.555
Is figure out how
we're doing on prevention,


01:17:06.555 --> 01:17:07.856
And we were able
to turn that around,


01:17:07.856 --> 01:17:11.326
So the likelihood that more and
more people were being infected


01:17:11.326 --> 01:17:13.495
Had--Had been changed.


01:17:13.495 --> 01:17:15.664
So less despair.


01:17:15.664 --> 01:17:19.568
Less sense of absolute crisis.


01:17:20.002 --> 01:17:22.170
We're now
getting into a sense of


01:17:22.170 --> 01:17:25.641
Maybe there's
a place to go here.


01:17:25.641 --> 01:17:28.243
- Some things
seemed to be working.


01:17:28.243 --> 01:17:30.412
I'm not saying
that there was a cure,


01:17:30.412 --> 01:17:34.316
But there was a slow down.


01:17:34.750 --> 01:17:38.220
You know, people weren't
dropping like flies anymore.


01:17:38.654 --> 01:17:43.425
Some people were, uh,
hanging on.


01:17:43.859 --> 01:17:47.329
There was this one guy.


01:17:47.329 --> 01:17:48.630
He was in a wheelchair.


01:17:49.064 --> 01:17:50.365
He used to come
by in a bicycle,


01:17:50.365 --> 01:17:51.667
And then
he was in a wheelchair,


01:17:51.667 --> 01:17:53.402
And then he had a patch
over his eye,


01:17:53.402 --> 01:17:56.004
And i really hated
to look at him,


01:17:56.004 --> 01:17:59.041
Because i remember
when this guy


01:17:59.041 --> 01:18:00.776
Used to come by on his bicycle


01:18:01.209 --> 01:18:03.812
And buy flowers for his sister,


01:18:03.812 --> 01:18:06.415
And we would just laugh
and everything,


01:18:06.848 --> 01:18:09.017
And i couldn't
laugh at him anymore,


01:18:09.017 --> 01:18:11.186
Because he was coming by
in a wheelchair,


01:18:11.186 --> 01:18:13.789
And it was like he was
almost on his way out,


01:18:13.789 --> 01:18:18.994
And i just thought,
"god, where are you?


01:18:18.994 --> 01:18:22.464
Look at what's happening."


01:18:22.464 --> 01:18:25.067
And he was one of the first


01:18:25.500 --> 01:18:28.537
Who the next time i saw him,


01:18:28.537 --> 01:18:30.272
He wasn't in a wheelchair.


01:18:30.272 --> 01:18:32.874
He was walking.
He had a cane.


01:18:32.874 --> 01:18:34.609
And then the next
time i saw him,


01:18:34.609 --> 01:18:37.646
He didn't have that
eye patch on anymore.


01:18:38.080 --> 01:18:41.983
And then,
hey, i swear to you,


01:18:41.983 --> 01:18:45.454
Yesterday,
i saw him at my flower stand.


01:18:45.887 --> 01:18:48.056
On his bicycle.
And he was back.


01:18:48.056 --> 01:18:50.659
He wasn't back like he was
in the beginning,


01:18:50.659 --> 01:18:54.996
But, you know, i'm not the way
i was 20 years ago either,


01:18:54.996 --> 01:19:01.503
But he was there, and he had
gone through the storm,


01:19:01.503 --> 01:19:04.106
And he had
weathered the storm,


01:19:04.106 --> 01:19:08.877
And his spirit was just
as bright and effervescent


01:19:08.877 --> 01:19:11.046
As it was in the beginning.


01:19:13.215 --> 01:19:14.950
-The washington post
came out with a headline,


01:19:15.383 --> 01:19:17.119
And it showed death from aids,


01:19:17.119 --> 01:19:19.287
And it was a graph
going down.


01:19:19.287 --> 01:19:23.191
And it basically said
"cocktail proves effective


01:19:23.191 --> 01:19:25.794
Against hiv aids."


01:19:25.794 --> 01:19:30.999
This means that aids work
as we know it is transformed.


01:19:48.784 --> 01:19:50.952
- I remember my friend ben
saying in the old days


01:19:50.952 --> 01:19:52.687
That he would never
go to costco


01:19:52.687 --> 01:19:55.290
And buy one of those
big things of toilet paper,


01:19:55.724 --> 01:19:57.893
'Cause he didn't think
he'd ever use it all up,


01:19:57.893 --> 01:20:00.495
And now he can.
That's the difference.


01:20:00.929 --> 01:20:03.098
I would never take a commission


01:20:03.098 --> 01:20:04.833
More than five
or six months out,


01:20:04.833 --> 01:20:07.002
'Cause i didn't think
i'd be able to finish it.


01:20:07.435 --> 01:20:11.773
Now i'll take a commission
that's, you know, a year out.


01:20:11.773 --> 01:20:14.810
And now i have
a partner to my love,


01:20:14.810 --> 01:20:16.545
And whom i hope to be with


01:20:16.978 --> 01:20:18.280
For a very, very long time,


01:20:18.280 --> 01:20:20.448
And so i'm imagining a future.


01:20:20.448 --> 01:20:22.617
I'm allowing myself
to imagine a future.


01:20:23.051 --> 01:20:24.786
And that's--
That's scary too.


01:20:24.786 --> 01:20:26.955
There's still--I mean,
i can feel it right now.


01:20:26.955 --> 01:20:28.690
There's, like,
butterflies in my stomach,


01:20:28.690 --> 01:20:29.558
'Cause, like, i'm hoping.


01:20:29.558 --> 01:20:31.726
I'm feeling that hope again.


01:20:31.726 --> 01:20:33.028
And i could lose it.


01:20:33.461 --> 01:20:34.763
And i have
to remember that.


01:20:34.763 --> 01:20:37.799
'Cause, you know,
you get sick, and bam.


01:20:37.799 --> 01:20:41.703
You just sink
right down again.


01:20:45.173 --> 01:20:48.210
- My friend john
who has studied buddhism


01:20:48.210 --> 01:20:52.113
Talks about
this metaphor of people


01:20:52.113 --> 01:20:56.885
Who have been through
some huge experience of loss,


01:20:56.885 --> 01:21:01.223
Who cannot find their way back,
if you will,


01:21:01.223 --> 01:21:03.391
To the land of the living.


01:21:03.391 --> 01:21:05.560
But they still walk the earth.


01:21:05.994 --> 01:21:09.464
Hungry.
Hungry for connection.


01:21:09.464 --> 01:21:16.404
Hungry for some way to regain
a sense of life and balance,


01:21:16.404 --> 01:21:19.007
And that--I do,


01:21:19.007 --> 01:21:20.742
When i walk through
the castro sometimes,


01:21:20.742 --> 01:21:24.646
I see--I see people who
haven't been able to do that,


01:21:24.646 --> 01:21:27.682
And that's something that could
have easily happened to me,


01:21:27.682 --> 01:21:29.417
And that i could have,
you know,


01:21:29.417 --> 01:21:32.020
Become one of those
hungry ghosts,


01:21:32.020 --> 01:21:35.056
And, luckily, for me...


01:21:38.526 --> 01:21:41.563
it changed.
I met someone.


01:21:41.563 --> 01:21:44.599
And i encountered life again.


01:21:44.599 --> 01:21:47.636
Here was this man
walking down the street,


01:21:47.636 --> 01:21:52.407
And thank god i got it together,
and i said hello.


01:21:52.407 --> 01:21:55.877
And he's younger than me.


01:21:55.877 --> 01:21:58.480
Like,
much younger than me.


01:21:58.480 --> 01:22:02.817
And it's been a powerful,
powerful experience


01:22:02.817 --> 01:22:05.420
To love and be
very close to someone


01:22:05.420 --> 01:22:08.023
Who's younger than me
who did not have


01:22:08.023 --> 01:22:11.927
The experience that i had
with the aids epidemic


01:22:12.360 --> 01:22:14.095
And all that terrible loss,


01:22:14.529 --> 01:22:18.433
And go on with my life
having that inside me,


01:22:18.433 --> 01:22:25.373
And it not be
the all-Consuming...


01:22:25.373 --> 01:22:27.976
experience that i had had.


01:22:27.976 --> 01:22:31.446
And as much
as i think about my father


01:22:31.446 --> 01:22:33.181
And what he went through
in the war,


01:22:33.181 --> 01:22:37.519
I don't want, like,
my war to do to me


01:22:37.519 --> 01:22:39.688
What it did to him.


01:22:44.025 --> 01:22:47.495
- In january of 2007,
i became the executive director


01:22:47.929 --> 01:22:52.701
Of the glbt historical society
in san francisco.


01:22:52.701 --> 01:22:55.737
And, uh,
and it surprised me


01:22:55.737 --> 01:22:58.340
That basically
the conversation about aids


01:22:58.340 --> 01:23:00.075
That i'd been having
for so many years


01:23:00.075 --> 01:23:02.243
Wasn't still going on
in that group,


01:23:02.243 --> 01:23:05.714
Or in the community--The glbt
community of san francisco.


01:23:05.714 --> 01:23:07.015
Because, for me,
it had continued,


01:23:07.015 --> 01:23:08.750
'Cause i was doing
international aids work


01:23:08.750 --> 01:23:10.051
And working with aids groups.


01:23:10.051 --> 01:23:12.220
So suddenly no one
was talking about aids.


01:23:12.220 --> 01:23:13.521
There weren't people with aids


01:23:13.521 --> 01:23:15.256
Who everyone was sort of--


01:23:15.690 --> 01:23:17.425
If they were around,
they were--


01:23:17.425 --> 01:23:19.160
Took me a while to figure out
who they were,


01:23:19.594 --> 01:23:25.233
And, uh, an entire,
you know, part of, uh...


01:23:25.233 --> 01:23:28.703
how i had perceived
the community had changed.


01:23:32.607 --> 01:23:35.210
- I don't have to worry
when i'm old,


01:23:35.210 --> 01:23:36.945
You know,
and looking back at my life


01:23:37.379 --> 01:23:39.114
That i didn't do anything.


01:23:39.114 --> 01:23:41.716
And in terms of my politics,


01:23:42.150 --> 01:23:47.355
This was the thing
that i got to do the most.


01:23:49.958 --> 01:23:52.560
Without all these people
participating


01:23:52.994 --> 01:23:54.295
In these clinical trials,


01:23:54.295 --> 01:23:57.332
We would not be
where we are today.


01:23:57.332 --> 01:23:59.934
And i really wish


01:23:59.934 --> 01:24:05.140
That some of them
were around today.


01:24:05.140 --> 01:24:07.308
To see where we are.


01:24:07.308 --> 01:24:09.477
Because...
i don't know.


01:24:09.477 --> 01:24:12.080
They just gave a lot.


01:24:15.984 --> 01:24:19.454
- This tragedy,
it taught us how to be humble.


01:24:19.454 --> 01:24:21.189
It taught us
how to be honest.


01:24:21.189 --> 01:24:24.225
It taught us how to--
To love


01:24:24.225 --> 01:24:28.129
In spite of what's
at the end of the tunnel.


01:24:28.563 --> 01:24:32.901
You know, how to be
a little bit more considerate


01:24:33.334 --> 01:24:35.503
Of another person.


01:24:35.503 --> 01:24:39.841
It--It showed us
how to find spirituality.


01:24:39.841 --> 01:24:42.444
It taught me.
I can only speak for myself.


01:24:42.877 --> 01:24:45.046
It taught me
how to find my spirit,


01:24:45.480 --> 01:24:48.083
And how to, you know,


01:24:48.083 --> 01:24:51.119
Make my flame brighter.


01:24:55.023 --> 01:24:58.493
- You know, it's, like,
the aids epidemic is not over.


01:24:58.927 --> 01:25:02.397
I still have friends
who are living with hiv.


01:25:02.397 --> 01:25:06.301
Every once in a while,
someone i know becomes infected.


01:25:06.734 --> 01:25:08.903
I mean, it continues.


01:25:08.903 --> 01:25:12.373
What has stopped continuing,


01:25:12.373 --> 01:25:15.844
At least in san francisco
and in most of the--


01:25:15.844 --> 01:25:17.579
Of the developed world,


01:25:17.579 --> 01:25:23.651
Is the--The vast amount
of sickness and death.


01:25:24.085 --> 01:25:29.724
I would really like
to be able to live long enough


01:25:29.724 --> 01:25:33.194
To know, like,
how does the epidemic


01:25:33.194 --> 01:25:34.929
Actually come to an end?


01:25:35.363 --> 01:25:37.098
Like,
will the treatments come


01:25:37.532 --> 01:25:41.870
And--And finally
and effectively stop people


01:25:41.870 --> 01:25:43.171
From becoming sicker?


01:25:43.605 --> 01:25:47.075
And will the vaccine come,
and--


01:25:47.075 --> 01:25:51.846
And stop people from being able
to transmit and acquire it,


01:25:52.280 --> 01:25:56.618
And--And will it all
just finally...


01:25:56.618 --> 01:25:59.654
finally just stop?


01:26:02.257 --> 01:26:04.859
- You know, when people say,
"how did you get through it?"


01:26:04.859 --> 01:26:07.028
It's like,
"i don't know."


01:26:07.028 --> 01:26:09.197
You know?
You just do.


01:26:09.197 --> 01:26:10.064
And everybody does.


01:26:10.498 --> 01:26:12.667
I mean, anybody
who's got cancer or aids,


01:26:12.667 --> 01:26:15.270
And they're just like, "oh,
you're, you know, so amazing


01:26:15.270 --> 01:26:16.571
You--You've
gotten through this."


01:26:16.571 --> 01:26:19.174
It's like,
"do i have a choice?"


01:26:19.174 --> 01:26:20.909
You know.
I want to stay alive,


01:26:20.909 --> 01:26:23.077
And i'm gonna take care
of myself the best i can,


01:26:23.511 --> 01:26:26.548
And you just do it.
And it's not heroic.


01:26:26.548 --> 01:26:28.716
You just do it.


01:26:28.716 --> 01:26:31.753
And the same thing
with losing a partner.


01:26:31.753 --> 01:26:33.054
It's...


01:26:33.054 --> 01:26:36.090
you know,
so many, you know...


01:26:36.090 --> 01:26:38.259
most people in the world
lose partners,


01:26:38.693 --> 01:26:39.994
You know, at one time
in their lives or another,


01:26:40.428 --> 01:26:42.163
And you just--
You live through it,


01:26:42.163 --> 01:26:43.898
And it's horrible,


01:26:43.898 --> 01:26:47.368
But you do live through it.


01:26:49.971 --> 01:26:55.176
I know i have so many friends
who died so young.


01:26:55.176 --> 01:26:56.911
That's--I mean, that's...


01:26:57.345 --> 01:27:01.683
that, to me,
is the most painful part.


01:27:01.683 --> 01:27:04.719
What would the world be like now
if they were alive?


01:27:05.153 --> 01:27:07.755
It would be different.
It would be very different.


01:27:08.189 --> 01:27:10.358
I mean,
so many powerful people,


01:27:10.358 --> 01:27:12.527
Talented people.


01:27:15.997 --> 01:27:19.901
I miss...


01:27:19.901 --> 01:27:21.636
i miss a lot of them.


01:27:21.636 --> 01:27:24.672
A lot.


01:27:26.841 --> 01:27:30.311
Captioning by ryan
atcaptionmax

